B.aL8=new A.k("Initial Presentation","You are the attending physiatrist on a dedicated acute brain injury unit. You are paged to evaluate a 24-year-old male who is 3 weeks status-post severe traumatic brain injury (GCS 6 at scene, 4-day LOC). Over the preceding 24 hours, the patient has demonstrated escalating irritability. The nursing staff reports an episode of physical aggression toward a therapist during morning mobilization. The patient is currently being managed with 1-on-1 supervision.")
B.aLn=new A.k(u.W,"In assessing this acute change in behavioral status, your history should focus on:\n- **Chronological Recovery**: Confirmation of GCS, duration of post-traumatic amnesia (PTA), and most recent Rancho Los Amigos level (Previously Ranchos V; currently appearing Ranchos IV).\n- **Safety & Restraint**: Evaluation of immediate risk to self/others and current behavioral containment strategies.\n- **Secondary Insults**: Assessment of potential triggers including sleep-wake cycle disruption, environmental overstimulation (new roommate, noise), or acute pain generators.\n- **Physiological Review**: Monitoring of bowel/bladder patterns (e.g., last bowel movement, urinary output/catheter status) and dietary intake.\n- **Medication Reconciliation**: Review of current neuro-pharmacotherapy (e.g., Levetiracetam, Enoxaparin, scheduled Acetaminophen).")
B.aOk=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vital Signs**: T 99.1\xb0F, HR 112 bpm, BP 145/90 mmHg, SpO2 96% on room air.\n- **General Observation**: The patient is hypervigilant, pacing the room, and demonstrates verbal disinhibition. He is disoriented to person, place, and time.\n- **Cognitive Assessment**: ABS (Agitated Behavior Scale) score is 32, consistent with severe agitation. Attention is severely impaired.\n- **Neurological**: Pupils are equal and reactive. Motor examination reveals symmetric movement of all four extremities, though limited by poor cooperation. Global hyperreflexia with sustained 2-beat clonus is noted bilaterally.\n- **Additional Systems**: Abdominal palpation reveals mild distention. No occult fractures or new pressure injuries are identified.")
B.aMK=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture is consistent with Rancho Los Amigos Level IV (Confused-Agitated). However, the acute onset and associated tachycardia necessitate the exclusion of superimposed medical delirium or secondary neurological complications.")
B.aPT=new A.k("+ Provide a structured differential diagnosis for this acute behavioral escalation.","A systematic approach includes:\n1.  **Direct Neurological Complications**: Progressive post-traumatic hydrocephalus, subclinical seizure activity, or delayed intracranial hemorrhage/re-bleed.\n2.  **Extracranial Medical Etiologies**: \n    - **Infection**: Urinary tract infection (catheter-associated) or pneumonia.\n    - **Metabolic/Endocrine**: Hyponatremia (SIADH/Cerebral Salt Wasting), hypoxia, or glycemic dysregulation.\n    - **Physiological Discomfort**: Fecal impaction (note 3-day history), urinary retention, or occult pain.\n3.  **Pharmacological Factors**: Adverse effects of Levetiracetam ('Keppra-rage'), medication withdrawal, or toxicity.\n4.  **Environmental**: Circadian rhythm disruption and sensory overstimulation.")
B.aQE=new A.k("+ Based on the objective findings, what is the most appropriate next step in management?","Management should prioritize stabilizing the patient while identifying the underlying etiology:\n1.  **Immediate De-escalation**: Implementation of a 'low-stimulation' protocol.\n2.  **Diagnostic Workup**: Obtain a bladder scan to assess for retention, and order stat labs (CBC, BMP, UA with culture). Perform a non-contrast CT Head to rule out neurosurgical emergencies.\n3.  **Interventional Relief**: If the bladder scan or abdominal exam suggests retention/impaction, perform immediate catheterization or bowel disimpaction.\n4.  **Pharmacotherapeutic Adjustment**: Evaluate the risk-benefit profile of switching Levetiracetam to an alternative mood-stabilizing anticonvulsant, such as Valproic Acid.")
B.aMI=new A.k("+ Challenge: Post-Traumatic Amnesia (PTA)","The patient's family requests a prognosis and an explanation for his aggression. How do you define PTA and describe its clinical assessment?")
B.aQf=new A.k("+ Challenge Answer","Post-Traumatic Amnesia (PTA) is a transient state following TBI characterized by disorientation and the inability to form continuous new memories. Agitation (Rancho IV) is a frequent biological component of this phase. Clinical assessment utilizing validated tools like the **GOAT (Galveston Orientation and Amnesia Test)** or **LOGAS** is essential. Resolution of PTA (typically 2 consecutive GOAT scores >75) is one of the most reliable predictors of long-term functional and vocational outcomes.")
B.aLC=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The diagnostic workup identifies urinary retention (600cc on bladder scan) and fecal impaction. Following targeted intervention, the patient's heart rate normalizes to 84 bpm and the ABS score improves to 22 (mild agitation).")
B.aP_=new A.k("+ What pharmacologic strategy is indicated to optimize long-term cognitive recovery in this patient?","1.  **Dopaminergic Augmentation**: Commencement of **Amantadine** (e.g., 100mg BID). Level 1 evidence supports its use to accelerate the rate of functional recovery during the early subacute phase of TBI.\n2.  **Behavioral Stabilization**: If agitation remains severe or safety-limiting, consider **Valproic Acid** or **Propranolol**. \n3.  **Pharmacologic Avoidance**: Strictly avoid **Benzodiazepines** and first-generation antipsychotics, as they may impair neuroplasticity, cause paradoxical agitation, and prolong PTA.")
B.aPz=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The treatment team expresses concern regarding staff safety and requests the application of four-point soft wrist restraints.")
B.aMo=new A.k("+ Discuss the clinical rationale regarding the use of physical restraints in the TBI population.","A **restraint-minimization** approach is standard of care. Physical restraints often exacerbate 'trapped' sensations, leading to increased agitation, autonomic dysregulation, and risk of injury. Preferred alternatives include:\n- **Environmental Control**: Use of a canopy 'Vail' or 'Posey' bed to provide a safe, enclosed environment.\n- **Staffing**: Utilization of 1-on-1 sitters or video monitoring.\n- **Secondary Protection**: Concealing IV sites with stockinette or 'tubigrip' sleeves rather than hand-restraint.\n- **Proximity**: Relocating the patient closer to high-traffic nursing areas for better supervision.")
B.aLy=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: A bedside nurse, feeling distressed by the patient's previous aggression, asks: 'Can we just use a heavy sedative like Thorazine? It's not fair to the staff to be at risk for a patient who is clearly just aggressive by nature.'")
B.aLA=new A.k("+ Provide a professional response that addresses staff safety while providing clinical education.","I would respond: 'I truly appreciate the difficulty of providing care under these circumstances; staff safety is a paramount concern. However, this behavior is a clinical manifestation of his brain injury\u2014specifically a loss of frontal lobe inhibitory control\u2014rather than his baseline personality. Over-sedating him with medications like Thorazine would unfortunately 'mask' his neurological recovery and likely prolong this agitated phase. Instead, let's implement a 'low-stim' protocol and I will optimize his medications to improve his internal calm without impairing his cognitive progress. We will hold a team huddle to ensure every staff member feels supported and has a safe exit strategy during care tasks.'")
B.aCl=s([B.aL8,B.aLn,B.aOk,B.aMK,B.aPT,B.aQE,B.aMI,B.aQf,B.aLC,B.aP_,B.aPz,B.aMo,B.aLy,B.aLA],t.E)
B.PI=new A.bk("agitation","Post-Traumatic Agitation",B.aCl,!1)
B.aMB=new A.k("Initial Presentation","You are consulted for post-operative rehabilitation recommendations for a 53-year-old male who is 2 days status-post Right Transtibial Amputation (BKA). The amputation was necessitated by a non-healing diabetic foot ulcer complicated by osteomyelitis.")
B.aNf=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","Your initial assessment should address the following clinical domains:\n- **Etiology & Comorbidities**: Evaluation of dysvascular risk factors (Diabetes mellitus with A1c 8.2%, HTN, HLD) and renal function (Stage 3 CKD with Cr 1.8).\n- **Premorbid Functional Status**: Detailed vocational and avocational history (e.g., mail carrier, 10-15 miles daily ambulation) and baseline independence in ADLs/IADLs.\n- **Pain Profile**: Categorization of pain as nociceptive (surgical site, 4/10) vs. neuropathic (phantom limb pain, 7/10).\n- **Psychosocial & Environment**: Assessment of home architecture (2-story, 15 steps) and available support systems.\n- **Secondary Prevention**: Status of the 'intact' contralateral limb, including history of neuropathy or previous ulceration.")
B.aOv=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Residual Limb**: Post-surgical dressing is in place. The incision is well-approximated with staples; no signs of dehiscence or infection. Tibial length is 15cm from the joint line.\n- **Contralateral Limb**: Diminished sensation to 10g monofilament in a stocking distribution. Pedal pulses (DP/PT) are 1+ bilaterally. No active integumentary issues.\n- **Vitals & Anthropometrics**: HR 88 bpm, BP 138/88 mmHg. BMI is 25.\n- **Neuromusculoskeletal**: Strength is 5/5 in UEs and L lower extremity (except 4/5 ankle dorsiflexion). R hip extension is restricted (-10 degrees flexion contracture).")
B.aP7=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical objective in this subacute phase is the management of post-operative edema, prevention of secondary joint contractures, and optimization of the residual limb for future prosthetic fitting.")
B.aN4=new A.k("+ Formulate the immediate post-operative management plan for this patient.","1.  **Limb Protection & Edema Control**: Transition from a soft dressing to a **Rigid Removable Dressing (RRD)**. This provides mechanical protection against falls, reduces edema, and facilitates frequent wound inspection.\n2.  **Contracture Prevention**: Implement a daily 'Prone Lying' protocol (20 mins TID) to address hip flexion contractures. Ensure the knee is maintained in extension while sitting (avoiding 'pillow-behind-the-knee' positioning).\n3.  **Phantom Pain Management**: Initiate **Gabapentin** for neuropathic phantom limb pain; begin education on non-pharmacological strategies like mirror therapy once the incision is stable.")
B.aMO=new A.k("+ Discuss the clinical significance of the 'intact' limb in a patient with Stage 3 CKD and peripheral neuropathy.","The 'intact' limb is at high risk for future limb loss (50% risk within 3-5 years post-primary amputation in dysvascular patients). Management must include:\n- **Tertiary Prevention**: Mandatory daily visual foot inspections and the use of high-quality, protective footwear (avoiding barefoot ambulation).\n- **Vascular Optimization**: Intensive glucose and blood pressure control to mitigate the progression of small-vessel disease and ensure adequate perfusion for surgical healing.")
B.aPy=new A.k("+ Challenge: Provide a K-Level assessment and justification for this patient.","Based on his premorbid function (15 miles/day) and vocational requirements (mail carrier), the patient is an appropriate candidate for a **K3 or K4 classification**. A **K3** designation is justified as he has the potential for ambulation with variable cadence, which is necessary for community navigation and vocational duties. This status is critical for justifying advanced prosthetic components like energy-storing feet.")
B.aPo=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT (Subacute Follow-up)","The patient is now 6 months post-amputation. The residual limb is mature, well-healed, and non-edematous. He is ready for a definitive prosthetic prescription.")
B.aKj=new A.k("+ Provide a detailed definitive prosthetic prescription for this K3-level transtibial amputee.","The prescription should include:\n1.  **Socket**: Total Surface Bearing (TSB) or Patellar Tendon Bearing (PTB) socket with a flexible inner liner.\n2.  **Suspension**: **Suction or Elevated Vacuum suspension**. This is superior to a locking pin for high-activity users as it reduces 'pistoning' and improves proprioceptive feedback during community ambulation.\n3.  **Componentry**: Lightweight pylon and an **Energy Storage and Return (ESR) carbon fiber foot** with multi-axial capability to accommodate uneven terrain encountered during vocational tasks.")
B.aLo=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The third-party payer has denied the request for the ESR foot, stating that a basic SACH foot meets the minimum requirements for household ambulation.")
B.aKm=new A.k("+ How do you structure a 'Letter of Medical Necessity' (CMN) to appeal this denial?","The appeal must emphasize 'Functional Requirement' over 'Minimum Necessity':\n- **Vocational Data**: Document the requirement for variable cadence and high-mileage ambulation for his job as a mail carrier.\n- **Secondary Prevention**: Argue that advanced componentry (ESR/Multi-axial) reduces compensatory forces on the intact limb and lumbar spine, thereby preventing future multi-segmental MSK injuries and associated costs.\n- **Environmental safety**: Detail the need for multi-axial stability to navigate the 'uneven terrain' inherent in community and vocational settings.")
B.aP8=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: During a 3-week follow-up, the patient expresses frustration: 'I saw an athlete on TV with a computerized leg running 5 days after his accident. Why am I stuck in this sock? I feel like my recovery is being mismanaged.'")
B.aKN=new A.k("+ Provide a professional response that addresses the patient's concerns while managing clinical expectations.","I would say: 'I understand your frustration; seeing rapid recoveries online can make your own process feel slow. However, the 'computerized' legs you see require a very stable, maturely shaped limb to fit correctly. Because your amputation was caused by circulation issues and diabetes, we have to be much more careful with your skin's healing. Fitting a socket too early would lead to skin breakdown, which could set your progress back by months. We are currently building the foundation so that you can safely use those advanced components for the 15 miles a day you aspire to walk. We aren't holding you back; we are ensuring your recovery is durable and successful.'")
B.aCu=s([B.aMB,B.aNf,B.aOv,B.aP7,B.aN4,B.aMO,B.aPy,B.aPo,B.aKj,B.aLo,B.aKm,B.aP8,B.aKN],t.E)
B.PK=new A.bk("amputee-care","Post-Amputation Rehabilitation",B.aCu,!1)
B.aNq=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 55-year-old female presents with progressive swelling in her right arm. She has a history of Right Breast Cancer treated with lumpectomy, axillary lymph node dissection, and radiation therapy 2 years ago.")
B.aMN=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a cancer survivor with new limb swelling, you must rule out **Malignancy** and **DVT** before assuming Lymphedema.\n- **Red Flags**: Rapid onset? Pain? (Lymphedema is usually painless/heavy). Fever/Erythema? (Cellulitis).\n- **Cancer History**: Receptor status? Nodal status? (More nodes removed = Higher risk).\n- **Functional**: heaviness, jewelry tightness, clothing fit.")
B.aNv=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Right arm is visibly larger than Left. No erythema or wounds.\n- **Palpation**: **Stemmer's Sign Positive** (Inability to pinch a skin fold at the base of the second digit). Texture is fibrotic (firm).\n- **Measurements**: >2cm difference in circumference at multiple landmarks.\n- **Vascular**: Pulses intact. Capillary refill <3s.")
B.aM6=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture (Painless, progressive swelling, Stemmer positive, history of radiation/node dissection) is classic for **Stage 2 Secondary Lymphedema**.")
B.aQy=new A.k("+ Define the International Society of Lymphology (ISL) Staging.","1.  **Stage 0 (Latent)**: Lymph transport impaired, but no visible swelling. Patient feels 'heaviness'.\n2.  **Stage 1 (Reversible)**: Visible swelling that **pits** and **reduces with elevation**.\n3.  **Stage 2 (Spontaneously Irreversible)**: Swelling does **not** reduce with elevation. Skin becomes fibrotic/hard (current patient).\n4.  **Stage 3 (Lymphostatic Elephantiasis)**: Extreme swelling, skin changes (papillomas, hyperkeratosis).")
B.aNS=new A.k("+ You order a Doppler Ultrasound to rule out DVT (Negative). What is the Gold Standard treatment?","**Complete Decongestive Therapy (CDT)**. It has two phases:\n1.  **Phase 1 (Decongestion)**: Daily therapy for 2-4 weeks.\n    - Manual Lymphatic Drainage (MLD).\n    - Short-Stretch Bandaging (Low resting pressure, high working pressure).\n    - Skin Care & Exercise.\n2.  **Phase 2 (Maintenance)**: Lifetime.\n    - Compression Garments (Daytime).\n    - Self-MLD.\n    - Nighttime bandaging.")
B.aPF=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You prescribe CDT. Two weeks later, she presents with sudden onset redness, warmth, and high fever (102\xb0F) in the lymphedematous arm.")
B.aM3=new A.k("+ What is the diagnosis and immediate management?","- **Diagnosis**: **Cellulitis** (Dermatolymphangioadenitis). Lymphedema fluid is protein-rich ('stagnant pond'), making it a perfect culture medium for bacteria (Strep/Staph).\n- **Management**: \n    1. **Stop CDT**: Mechanical compression could spread the infection (Sepsis risk).\n    2. **Antibiotics**: Immediate systemic antibiotics.\n    3. **Resume CDT**: Only after signs of infection have resolved (pain/erythema/fever gone).")
B.aMt=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","She asks: 'Can I have my blood pressure taken or an IV line in this arm? My other arm has bad veins.'")
B.aKL=new A.k("+ Provide a Risk-Reduction recommendation.","**Lymphedema Risk Reduction Practices**:\n- **Avoid**: Needle sticks, IVs, Blood Pressure cuffs, and constrictive jewelry on the affected limb.\n- **Rationale**: Any trauma or constriction can trigger a worsening of the lymphedema or introduce bacteria (Cellulitis).\n- **Nuance**: In an *Emergency* (Code Blue), life over limb. But for routine care, use the contralateral arm or a leg.")
B.aL1=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient is crying. 'My arm looks huge. I feel like a monster. I survived cancer but now I have to wear this ugly sleeve forever?'")
B.aKX=new A.k("+ Manage this Body Image distress.","1.  **Validate**: 'It is completely unfair. You fought so hard to beat the cancer, and this feels like a constant reminder of it. Your feelings of frustration are valid.'\n2.  **Reframing**: 'The sleeve is not a sign of sickness, but a tool for **empowerment**. It keeps your arm healthy so you can lift your grandkids/play tennis/work. We can also look at different colors/patterns (e.g., tattoo sleeves) to make it feel more like *you*.'\n3.  **Support**: Offer referral to a Cancer Survivorship Support Group.")
B.aBs=s([B.aNq,B.aMN,B.aNv,B.aM6,B.aQy,B.aNS,B.aPF,B.aM3,B.aMt,B.aKL,B.aL1,B.aKX],t.E)
B.PF=new A.bk("armswelling","Cancer Rehabilitation (Lymphedema)",B.aBs,!1)
B.aLJ=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 54-year-old female presents to your outpatient clinic with a 2-month history of symmetric proximal girdle aching. She describes localized discomfort in both shoulders and hips, which is most debilitating in the early morning. She reports significant difficulty performing overhead tasks, such as grooming and retrieving objects from high shelves, specifically during the first two hours after waking.")
B.aQe=new A.k(u.W,"In your evaluation of symmetric proximal aching, you should prioritize the following:\n- **Diurnal Variation**: Characterization of the duration and severity of morning stiffness (currently reported as >60 minutes) and its response to activity.\n- **Systemic Symptoms**: Evaluation for constitutional signs, including low-grade pyrexia, unintentional weight loss (5 lbs), and exertional fatigue.\n- **Neurological/Vascular Red Flags**: Screening for symptoms of Giant Cell Arteritis (GCA), including new-onset localized headaches, jaw claudication during mastication, or transient visual obscurations.\n- **Functional Impairment**: Assessment of ADL interference (e.g., dressing, driving, hair care) and response to previous conservative measures (PRN Ibuprofen with minimal benefit).")
B.aKd=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Musculoskeletal (Shoulders)**: \n    - **Passive Range of Motion (PROM)**: Near full and symmetric in all planes, suggesting an extra-articular process.\n    - **Active Range of Motion (AROM)**: Restricted by pain, specifically during abduction and external rotation; 'painful arc' noted bilaterally.\n    - **Strength Testing**: Demonstrates 4/5 effort in the deltoids and supraspinatus bilaterally. Weakness appears to be secondary to 'reflexic inhibition' (pain-limited) rather than a primary neurologic or myopathic process.\n- **Axial Skeleton**: Cervical spine ROM is full; negative Spurling\u2019s maneuver. Temporal arteries are non-tender and demonstrate normal pulsatility without thickening.\n- **Lower Extremities**: Symmetric aching in the proximal thighs; gait is slow but mechanically stable.")
B.aPR=new A.k("+ Challenge: Provide the classic musculoskeletal ultrasound (MSK-US) findings associated with this clinical presentation.","A key diagnostic feature of Polymyalgia Rheumatica (PMR) on MSK-US includes **bilateral subacromial-subdeltoid (SASD) bursitis**. Additional findings often include biceps tenosynovitis and occasional glenohumeral or trochanteric synovitis.")
B.aKi=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Laboratory evaluation reveals an Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR) of 85 mm/hr and a C-Reactive Protein (CRP) of 4.2 mg/dL. A Complete Blood Count (CBC) demonstrates a mild normocytic, normochromic anemia (Hgb 11.2 g/dL).")
B.aMP=new A.k("+ What is your definitive diagnosis and initial therapeutic intervention?","The clinical and laboratory profile is diagnostic for **Polymyalgia Rheumatica (PMR)**. The most appropriate initial intervention is a low-dose corticosteroid trial (e.g., **Prednisone 15mg daily**). A dramatic clinical response\u2014often defined as >75% symptom resolution within 48 to 72 hours\u2014serves as both a therapeutic and diagnostic confirmation.")
B.aQ1=new A.k("+ What 'Secondary Prevention' measures are indicated for a patient initiating long-term corticosteroid therapy?","Given the anticipated 12\u201318 month treatment course, management must include:\n1.  **Skeleton Preservation**: Baseline **DEXA scan** to assess bone mineral density. Initiate Calcium (1200mg/day) and Vitamin D (800\u20131000 IU/day). Assess the need for bisphosphonate therapy based on FRAX scores or BMD results.\n2.  **Metabolic Surveillance**: Regular monitoring of blood pressure, fasting plasma glucose/HbA1c (for steroid-induced hyperglycemia), and a baseline ophthalmologic exam to monitor for cataract or glaucoma development.")
B.aK9=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Despite adherence to the prednisone taper, the patient eventually develops symptoms consistent with Giant Cell Arteritis (GCA) and suffers significant visual impairment in the right eye.")
B.aNG=new A.k("+ Formulate a comprehensive rehabilitation and community integration plan for this patient with new-onset visual impairment.","Rehabilitation should focus on safety and functional adaptation:\n- **Tertiary Prevention**: Referral to an Occupational Therapist specializing in **Low Vision Rehabilitation** for home safety modifications (e.g., high-contrast labeling, tactile markers).\n- **Vocational/Community Support**: Coordination with state organizations for the blind for 'Orientation and Mobility' training, including the use of assistive technology or white cane training if necessary.\n- **Psychosocial Intervention**: Screening and referral for professional counseling to manage the grief and adjustment disorder associated with sudden sensory loss.")
B.aND=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient is visibly distressed and states: 'I feel completely failed by this system. You told me it was just shoulder aching, and now I am blind in my right eye. How could you have missed this?'")
B.aLQ=new A.k("+ Provide a professional and empathetic response that addresses the patient's concerns while maintaining clinical boundaries.","I would respond: 'I deeply regret that this has been the outcome of your condition, and I can only imagine how devastating this vision loss is for you. To address your concern, at our initial consultation, we specifically screened for the markers of the vision-threatening condition (GCA)\u2014including checking your temporal pulses and asking about headaches or jaw pain\u2014and those tests were normal at that time. PMR and GCA are closely linked, and unfortunately, even with appropriate treatment for the shoulder pain, GCA can emerge subsequently. My immediate priority is to ensure we give you every resource available to adapt to this change and to coordinate closely with your rheumatologist to prevent further complications.'")
B.aC0=s([B.aLJ,B.aQe,B.aKd,B.aPR,B.aKi,B.aMP,B.aQ1,B.aK9,B.aNG,B.aND,B.aLQ],t.E)
B.PT=new A.bk("bilateralshoulderpain","Inflammatory Proximal Girdle Aching",B.aC0,!1)
B.aN9=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 48-year-old male with a history of C8 ASIA A traumatic spinal cord injury (SCI) sustained 3 years ago presents to your clinic with a 3-month history of progressive bilateral shoulder pain and feeling 'weaker' during his daily manual wheelchair propulsion. He notes that the pain is worse when he is pushing up ramps or during car transfers.")
B.aQr=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","To evaluate this new symptom in the context of chronic SCI, your history should address:\n- **Etiology of Injury**: History of the initial trauma (surfing accident) and subsequent stability of his neurologic level.\n- **Pain Characterization**: Differentiation between musculoskeletal (nociceptive) pain and neuropathic 'central' pain.\n- **Neurologic Review**: Changes in bowel/bladder function, new onset of spasticity, or autonomic dysreflexia symptoms.\n- **Sensory Symptoms**: Specifically inquire about loss of temperature sensation (e.g., accidentally burning hands on coffee or hot water) despite preserved light touch.\n- **Functional Assessment**: Use of assistive technology (manual wheelchair) and vocational requirements (Post Office employee).")
B.aOC=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Neurologic (UE)**: \n    - **Strength**: Baseline C8 ASIA A; however, he now demonstrates new 4/5 weakness in elbow extension (C7) and elbow flexion (C5/C6) which was previously 5/5. Finger flexors (C8) are 3/5.\n    - **Sensation**: **Dissociated Sensory Loss** noted in a 'cape-like' distribution over the shoulders and upper back. He has significantly diminished pain/temperature sensation (pinprick), but light touch and vibration remain intact.\n    - **LMN Signs**: Mild visible atrophy and occasional fasciculations in the bilateral triceps.\n- **Neurologic (LE)**: Stable 0/5 strength; however, he demonstrates **increased hyperreflexia** (4+ patellar/achilles) and sustained ankle clonus, which is an increase from his usual baseline spasticity.\n- **Musculoskeletal**: Positive Hawkins and Neer signs bilaterally, initially suggesting impingement.")
B.aMm=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture of progressive weakness, increased spasticity below the level of injury, and dissociated sensory loss (cape-like distribution) in a patient with chronic SCI is highly concerning for a progressive intramedullary expansion.")
B.aN0=new A.k(u.u,"The differential must account for both localized MSK and central neurologic processes:\n1.  **Post-Traumatic Syringomyelia**: The primary concern given the dissociated sensory loss and progressive neurologic change.\n2.  **Repetitive Strain Injury (MSK)**: Subacromial impingement or rotator cuff tendinopathy (common in manual wheelchair users).\n3.  **Cervical Spondylotic Myelopathy**: Degenerative changes above the site of the original injury.\n4.  **Tethered Cord Syndrome**: Often presents with progressive neurologic loss or worsening spasticity.\n5.  **Neuropathic Pain**: Development of central pain syndromes.")
B.aQ9=new A.k("+ What are the most appropriate diagnostic imaging steps, and what is the specific target of your concern?","1.  **MRI of the Total Spine**: Specifically focusing on the cervical and upper thoracic regions. The target is to identify a **post-traumatic syrinx** (intramedullary cystic expansion).\n2.  **Shoulder X-rays**: To evaluate for glenohumeral degenerative changes, though unlikely to explain the 'cape-like' sensory loss.")
B.aOH=new A.k("+ Imaging identifies a cystic intramedullary expansion from C5-T2 (Syringomyelia). What is the definitive management approach?","- **Neurosurgical Consultation**: Referral for evaluation of possible surgical intervention (e.g., shunting, decompression, or drainage). \n- **Activity Modification**: Immediate cessation of high-impact or straining activities (such as heavy weightlifting or excessive wheelchair ramping) that may cause 'Valsalva' effects and exacerbate syrinx expansion.")
B.aOi=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is struggling to perform his job at the post office due to shoulder pain and weakness during wheelchair propulsion.")
B.aNL=new A.k("+ Beyond the neurologic referral, what assistive technology 'preservation strategy' would you propose to reduce the burden on his shoulders and prevent further decline?","I would recommend an evaluation for **Power-Assist Wheelchair Technology** (e.g., SmartDrive, E-Motion wheels, or a transition to a full power wheelchair). In long-term manual wheelchair users with SCI, preserving the upper extremities (the 'new' weight-bearing joints) is critical. Power-assist reduces the peak propulsion forces required for community navigation, thereby 'saving' the shoulders from further repetitive strain while his neurologic condition is managed.")
B.aKv=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient is fearful and says: 'Doc, I've already had one surgery that left me in a chair. I'm not letting anyone cut into my spinal cord again. I'll just do more PT and get stronger. I'll fight through the pain.'")
B.aLB=new A.k("+ Provide a professional response that addresses his fear while explaining the critical nature of the syrinx.","I would say: 'I completely hear your fear\u2014you've been through a major trauma and have worked incredibly hard to gain the independence you have now. My concern is that, unlike a muscle injury, this fluid in your spinal cord is essentially 'crowding out' the remaining healthy nerves. If we just try to 'work through it' with exercise, the pressure from the fluid can cause **irreversible** damage to the nerves that control your arm strength and sensation. Exercise won't fix this build-up; it might actually make it worse. We aren't rushing into surgery today, but we need to speak with the neurosurgeon to see how we can *protect* the function you still have. Our goal is to make sure you don't lose any more independence.'")
B.aC_=s([B.aN9,B.aQr,B.aOC,B.aMm,B.aN0,B.aQ9,B.aOH,B.aOi,B.aNL,B.aKv,B.aLB],t.E)
B.PA=new A.bk("bilateralshoulderpain2","Progressive Neurologic Loss in Chronic SCI",B.aC_,!1)
B.aQo=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 48-year-old female is currently undergoing inpatient rehabilitation following a moderate traumatic brain injury (TBI) and a right-sided subdural hematoma (SDH). On hospital day 12, she reports new-onset, progressively worsening left calf discomfort.")
B.aLd=new A.k("Clinical Assessment & Risk Stratification:","Your initial evaluation of suspected Venous Thromboembolism (VTE) should address:\n- **Symptom Profile**: Onset, location, and the presence of associated pleuritic chest pain or dyspnea.\n- **Risk Factors**: Current mobilization status, adherence to chemical and mechanical thromboprophylaxis, and premorbid risk factors (e.g., smoking, oral contraceptives, or malignancy).\n- **Diagnostic Scoring**: Application of the **Wells Criteria** to determine pre-test probability of Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT).\n- **Physiological Review**: Screening for Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS) or signs of hemodynamic compromise (tachycardia, tachypnea, hypoxia).")
B.aLc=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vital Signs**: T 99.4\xb0F, HR 108 bpm (tachycardia), BP 118/72 mmHg, SpO2 94% on room air.\n- **Integumentary**: The left lower extremity demonstrates non-pitting edema from the foot to the mid-calf. There is a 3cm increase in calf circumference compared to the contralateral limb. No localized erythema or warmth is noted.\n- **Neuromusculoskeletal**: Strength is 4/5 in the left L4-S1 distribution (baseline per TBI). Passive ankle dorsiflexion eliciting calf pain (Homan's sign is non-specific but noted). Sensation is diminished in a stocking-like distribution bilaterally.")
B.aQh=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient's Wells Score is 3 (1 point for swelling of the entire leg, 1 point for calf swelling >3cm, 1 point for paralysis/immobilization), indicating a high pre-test probability for DVT.")
B.aO8=new A.k("+ Formulate the appropriate diagnostic workup for this patient.","1.  **Lower Extremity Imaging**: **Lower Extremity Venous Duplex Ultrasonography** (Compression ultrasound) to evaluate for proximal DVT.\n2.  **Pulmonary Evaluation**: Given the borderline hypoxia (94%) and tachycardia, order a **CTA Chest (PE Protocol)** or V/Q scan to exclude Pulmonary Embolism (PE).\n3.  **Laboratory Studies**: CBC with differential, BMP, and Coagulation profile (PT/INR, PTT).\n4.  **Neurological Monitoring**: STAT **non-contrast CT Head** to evaluate the stability of the known subdural hematoma before initiating anticoagulation.")
B.aKI=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Duplex ultrasonography confirms a left common femoral DVT. The CTA Chest is negative for PE. The CT Head reveals a stable right-sided SDH with a slight reduction in size compared to the scan 10 days prior. Neurosurgery has been consulted.")
B.aPI=new A.k("+ Discuss the clinical decision-making process for initiating anticoagulation in this patient.","This represents a high-stakes management dilemma. Per **ACCP/AANS guidelines**:\n1.  **Risk vs. Benefit**: The risk of VTE propagation/PE must be balanced against the risk of intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) expansion. \n2.  **Timing**: In patients with stable ICH (no expansion on 24-48 hour follow-up), therapeutic anticoagulation can typically be initiated 7\u201310 days post-injury. \n3.  **Agent Selection**: I would prefer a **Continuous Heparin Infusion (unfractionated)**. Its short half-life and rapid reversibility with protamine make it safer if acute neurological decline occurs compared to long-acting oral anticoagulants or LMWH.\n4.  **Alternative**: If the neurosurgical team deems the bleeding risk too high, or if the SDH remains unstable, I would proceed with the placement of an **IVC Filter** as a bridge until anticoagulation is safe.")
B.aLF=new A.k("+ Challenge: Provide the pros and cons of IV Unfractionated Heparin vs. Low Molecular Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin) in this subacute TBI patient.","- **Unfractionated Heparin (IVH)**: \n    - *Pros*: Rapid onset/offset; easily reversible; predictable in renal failure.\n    - *Cons*: Requires frequent monitoring (aPTT/Anti-Xa) and bedside titration; higher risk of Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT).\n- **Enoxaparin (LMWH)**:\n    - *Pros*: High bioavailability; no routine monitoring required; lower risk of HIT.\n    - *Cons*: Prolonged half-life (making it 'less ideal' if an emergency surgery is needed); more difficult to reverse partially with protamine.")
B.aLU=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Your hospital's Quality Improvement (QI) committee identifies a cluster of new-onset VTE cases originating from one specific referring acute care facility.")
B.aLk=new A.k("+ Describe how you would conduct a 'Root Cause Analysis' (RCA) to investigate this disparity.","I would lead a multidisciplinary investigation following these steps:\n1.  **Data Verification**: Perform a formal audit to confirm the statistical significance of the VTE cluster compared to other referring centers.\n2.  **Process Mapping**: Investigate the transition of care. Were DVT prophylaxis orders (chemical and mechanical) being discontinued during transfer or never initiated at the referring site?\n3.  **Clinical Audit**: Review the incidence of VTE prophylaxis contraindications in that specific patient cohort.\n4.  **Stakeholder Collaboration**: Facilitate a 'Plan-Do-Study-Act' (PDSA) cycle with the acute site's Trauma/Neurosurgery leads to standardize 'VTE prophylaxis bundles' during the transfer process.")
B.aMY=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient's father is irate: 'The other hospital missed this DVT! This is negligence and it could have killed her. I'm calling my lawyer to sue everyone involved in her care before she got to you.'")
B.aQB=new A.k("+ Provide a professional response that addresses the father's anger while maintaining professional standards and patient confidentiality.","I would respond: 'I completely recognize how frightening this news is, especially given everything your daughter has already endured. First, I do need to ensure that I have her formal permission to discuss these details with you. [Assume permission is granted]. Regarding your concerns about the previous hospital, it's important to understand that a DVT can develop even with perfect medical care, and it is a known complication of decreased mobility after a brain injury. Our primary focus now is that we have identified it, she is stable, and we are initiating the standard of care for her treatment. I would encourage you to maintain an open dialogue with her previous team if you have questions about their specific protocols, but my priority here is ensuring her safe recovery and transition into active rehabilitation.'")
B.aBC=s([B.aQo,B.aLd,B.aLc,B.aQh,B.aO8,B.aKI,B.aPI,B.aLF,B.aLU,B.aLk,B.aMY,B.aQB],t.E)
B.Q_=new A.bk("calfdiscomfort","VTE Management in Acute Intracranial Hemorrhage",B.aBC,!1)
B.aM8=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 57-year-old male with a history of hypertension and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus presents to your clinic with a 5-day history of progressively evolving 'leg heaviness' and difficulty with ambulation. He reports that he now requires a front-wheeled walker for stability, whereas he was previously independent without assistive devices.")
B.aKY=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In assessing acute progressive weakness, your evaluation should focus on:\n- **Onset & Progression**: Characterization of the weakness as ascending or descending and the rate of functional decline.\n- **Neurological Review**: Inquire about distal paresthesias, cranial nerve symptoms (e.g., dysphagia, diplopia), and autonomic dysfunction (e.g., palpitations, bowel/bladder changes).\n- **Antecedent Events**: Specifically screen for recent upper respiratory infections or gastrointestinal illness (e.g., Diarrhea/Campylobacter jejuni) within the preceding 4 weeks.\n- **Red Flags**: Screen for midline back pain or 'saddle' anesthesia that would suggest a compressive myelopathy or cauda equina syndrome.")
B.aLh=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vital Signs**: BP 150/92, HR 94, RR 18, SpO2 97% on room air.\n- **Gait Analysis**: Demonstrates a wide-based, unsteady gait requiring significant upper extremity support on a walker. Foot drop is noted bilaterally during the swing phase.\n- **Neurological (Motor)**: \n    - **Strength**: 1/5 strength in bilateral ankle dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, and great toe extension. Proximal strength (hip flexion/knee extension) is preserved at 4+/5.\n    - **Reflexes**: **Areflexia** noted at the bilateral Achilles; 1+ (diminished) at the patellae and upper extremities.\n    - **Sensation**: Mildly diminished light touch and vibration in a 'stocking' distribution to the mid-shin.")
B.aPV=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical triad of progressive, relatively symmetric ascending weakness, areflexia, and a recent history of diarrheal illness is highly suggestive of Guillain-Barr\xe9 Syndrome (GBS).")
B.aNO=new A.k("+ Formulate the clinical and laboratory workup for this patient based on the Brighton Criteria.","1.  **Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) Analysis**: Look for **albuminocytologic dissociation** (elevated protein with a normal white blood cell count). \n2.  **Electrodiagnostic (EDX) Studies**: Nerve conduction studies (NCS) and needle EMG to evaluate for demyelinating features.\n3.  **Neuroimaging**: MRI of the Brain and Spine (specifically the Conus/Cauda Equina) to exclude compressive lesions or nerve root enhancement (common in GBS).\n4.  **Respiratory Assessment**: **Serial NIF (Negative Inspiratory Force) and VC (Vital Capacity)** every 4\u20136 hours is mandatory to monitor for impending respiratory failure.")
B.aOA=new A.k("+ The CSF analysis reveals protein of 110 mg/dL and WBC of 2 cells/\xb5L. EDX is performed at day 5 and is unrevealing. How do you interpret these results?","- **CSF**: The findings are classic for albuminocytologic dissociation, supporting the diagnosis of AIDP.\n- **EDX Timing**: Nerve conduction studies are often normal within the first 7 days of symptoms. The earliest findings are typically **prolonged or absent F-wave latencies** (indicating proximal demyelination) and prolonged H-reflexes. Standard features like conduction block or slowed velocities may not appear until 10\u201314 days. I would recommend a repeat EDX in 1 week if the diagnosis remains in question.")
B.aKu=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient is diagnosed with the AIDP variant of GBS. You must now coordinate his acute management and eventual rehabilitation.")
B.aLs=new A.k("+ What are the definitive therapeutic options for GBS, and what is the role of corticosteroids?","1.  **Immunotherapy**: Either **IVIG** (0.4g/kg/day for 5 days) or **Plasmapheresis** (PE) are first-line treatments with equivalent efficacy. IVIG is often preferred due to ease of administration.\n2.  **Corticosteroids**: Clinical trials have consistently shown **no functional benefit** for corticosteroids (oral or IV) in the treatment of GBS/AIDP. They should be avoided unless another indication exists.\n3.  **Supportive Care**: ICU admission for monitoring of autonomic instability (e.g., arrhythmias, labile blood pressure) and respiratory status (NIF/VC).")
B.aPk=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You are managing this patient in an outpatient clinic. His weakness is progressing rapidly and he is beginning to experience mild 'breathlessness'.")
B.aPW=new A.k("+ Describe your process for ensuring a safe 'Transfer of Care' to an acute-level facility.","I would initiate an emergency medical transfer:\n1.  **Communication**: Directly contact the Emergency Department (ED) attending or the ICU intensivist to provide a 'warm handoff,' emphasizing the risk of imminent respiratory failure.\n2.  **Documentation**: Ensure all clinic notes and preliminary lab results (if available) are sent with the patient.\n3.  **Stabilization**: Maintain the patient in a monitored setting until EMS arrival, ensuring oxygen and basic life support measures are available.")
B.aKT=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: An Internal Medicine colleague calls you after the patient is admitted and says: 'Why didn't you give him a high-dose steroid bolus in the clinic? He's paralyzed! We're wasting time and you're letting him get worse.'")
B.aNA=new A.k("+ Provide a professional, evidence-based response while maintaining a collaborative relationship.","I would respond: 'I completely share your urgency in wanting to stop the progression of his weakness. However, the international guidelines and specifically the Cochrane reviews for Guillain-Barr\xe9 Syndrome have shown that corticosteroids do not improve functional outcomes and may actually delay recovery. The most effective step we can take is the immediate initiation of IVIG or plasmapheresis and serial monitoring of his respiratory mechanics (NIF/VC). Let's work together to ensure he gets his first dose of IVIG as soon as possible, as that is what will truly change his prognosis.'")
B.aC6=s([B.aM8,B.aKY,B.aLh,B.aPV,B.aNO,B.aOA,B.aKu,B.aLs,B.aPk,B.aPW,B.aKT,B.aNA],t.E)
B.PR=new A.bk("difficultywalking","Acute Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyradiculoneuropathy (AIDP)",B.aC6,!1)
B.aM4=new A.k("Initial Presentation","You are consulted for a 34-year-old female who is 14 days status-post severe traumatic brain injury (TBI) sustained in a high-velocity motor vehicle accident. She required a left emegecy decompressive craniectomy for an acute subdural hematoma and remains in the neuro-trauma ICU with a Disorder of Consciousness (DOC).")
B.aKn=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In assessing a patient with a Disorder of Consciousness, your evaluation should focus on:\n- **Neurological Trajectory**: Determination of sleep-wake cycles, spontaneous eye-opening, and any evidence of purposeful interaction or command-following (e.g., consistency of responses).\n- **Physiological Stability**: Monitoring of paroxysmal events, including tachycardia, hypertension, and pyrexia.\n- **Medication Review**: Identification of 'centrally-acting' medications (e.g., sedatives, GABA-agonists) that may be suppressing arousal.\n- **Surgical Status**: Status of the bone flap and any intracranial pressure (ICP) monitoring data.\n- **Secondary Complications**: Screening for occult pain (e.g., fractures, pressure injuries), infection, or VTE.")
B.aQa=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings (CRS-R Assessment):","- **Arousal**: Spontaneous eye-opening is present and sustained. No vocalization is noted.\n- **Arousal Protocol**: The patient requires deep pressure stimulation for consistent eye-opening.\n- **Communication**: No evidence of functional or non-functional communication.\n- **Visual Function**: Demonstrates **visual pursuit** (tracking a mirror) twice in 2 out of 4 trials; visual fixation is inconsistently noted. No startle response.\n- **Motor**: Retraction to noxious stimuli (localizes to pain intermittently). No evidence of functional object use.\n- **Clinical Status**: Currently febrile (102.1\xb0F), tachycardic (128 bpm), and hypertensive (165/95 mmHg) with significant diaphoresis and extensor posturing of the lower extremities.")
B.aQF=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Based on the visual pursuit (tracking), the patient meets the criteria for a **Minimally Conscious State (MCS Minus)** according to the Coma Recovery Scale-Revised (CRS-R).")
B.aKg=new A.k("+ Provide a structured differential diagnosis for the patient's acute autonomic instability.","The differential for paroxysmal tachycardia, hypertension, and pyrexia in the DOC population must include:\n1.  **Paroxysmal Sympathetic Hyperactivity (PSH)**: The primary suspicion given the TBI etiology and associated hypertonicity/diaphoresis.\n2.  **Systemic Infection**: Sepsis (Pneumonia, UTI, central line infection) or meningitis/ventriculitis.\n3.  **Venous Thromboembolism (VTE)**: Pulmonary Embolism (PE).\n4.  **Neurogenic Factors**: Intermittent hydrocephalus or progressive 'midbrain' compression.\n5.  **Physiological Triggers**: Urinary retention, fecal impaction, or undiagnosed occult fractures.")
B.aKl=new A.k("+ Formulate the diagnostic criteria and management goals for Paroxysmal Sympathetic Hyperactivity (PSH).","1.  **Diagnosis**: Utilize the **PSH Assessment Measure (PSH-AM)**, which combines the Clinical Feature Scale (tachycardia, HTN, tachypnea, pyrexia, diaphoresis, posturing) with the Diagnosis Likelihood Tool. A score >17 indicates a high probability of PSH.\n2.  **Management Goals**:\n    - **Trigger Identification**: Eliminate noxious stimuli (pain, impaction).\n    - **Abortive Therapy**: Use rapidly acting agents (e.g., Morphine or short-acting Benzodiazepines) to terminate an acute paroxysm.\n    - **Preventative/Maintenance Therapy**: \n        - **Propranolol**: Non-selective beta-blockade to reduce cardiovascular symptoms (crosses the blood-brain barrier).\n        - **Gabapentin**: Reduces afferent sensory triggers.\n        - **Clonidine**: Central alpha-2 agonist to reduce sympathetic outflow.\n        - **Bromocriptine**: Dopamine agonist for thermoregulation and sympathetic suppression.")
B.aKx=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You observe that the PM&R consult service is rarely engaged in the acute management of DOC patients at your trauma center, despite the high clinical need.")
B.aMs=new A.k("+ Describe an 'Outreach and Educational Initiative' to address this deficit in the 'Systems-Based' care of DOC patients.","1.  **Service Audit**: Quantify the volume of patients with TBI/Anoxia and the time-to-consultation for PM&R.\n2.  **Multi-disciplinary Huddles**: Initiate regular 'Neuro-Trauma Rounds' with ICU intensivists and neurosurgeons to establish standardized PM&R entry points.\n3.  **Educational Seminar**: Provide Grand Rounds or nursing in-services on the utility of early **CRS-R testing** and the pharmacological management of PSH/arousal in the acute setting.\n4.  **System Integration**: Incorporate PM&R consultation as a standard 'order set' for all patients with severe TBI upon ICU stabilization.")
B.aOu=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The Trauma service states: 'We don't want to place a PEG tube. She has an NG tube, and we're hopefull she'll wake up next week. We want you to take her to rehab today as she is.'")
B.aO6=new A.k("+ Provide a professional and advocacy-focused response regarding the timing of permanent enteral access.","I would respond: 'I completely share your hope for a rapid recovery; however, based on her current **CRS-R scores** and the severity of her autonomic 'storming', her neurological recovery window into meaningful oral intake is likely measured in weeks to months, not days. An NG tube is a temporary solution that increases the risk of sinusitis and nasal mucosal breakdown. By placing a PEG tube now, we ensure she has a **durable nutritional foundation** for the high metabolic demands of intensive rehabilitation. Most importantly, it allows her to spend her time in rehab focused on neuro-recovery and mobility rather than being delayed for procedural complications or malnutrition. I recommend we proceed with PEG placement prior to transfer to ensure the most seamless functional outcome.'")
B.aD1=s([B.aM4,B.aKn,B.aQa,B.aQF,B.aKg,B.aKl,B.aKx,B.aMs,B.aOu,B.aO6],t.E)
B.PG=new A.bk("disorderofconsciousness","Disorder of Consciousness and Paroxysmal Sympathetic Hyperactivity",B.aD1,!1)
B.aKe=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 45-year-old male presents for evaluation of a 1-week history of right-sided foot drop and lateral calf pain. He reports that the symptoms developed gradually after a week-long hiking and camping excursion in rugged terrain.")
B.aQA=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In assessing acute foot drop, your evaluation should prioritize the following:\n- **Temporal Evolution**: Onset (sudden vs. gradual) and progression of motor deficit.\n- **Pain Characterization**: Localization of pain (e.g., lumbar spine, hip, or fibular head) and presence of neuropathic qualities (burning, tingling).\n- **Neurological Review**: Changes in bowel/bladder function, saddle anesthesia, or systemic symptoms (fever, weight loss).\n- **Vocational/Avocational Activity**: Recent high-mileage ambulation, kneeling, or weight loss (which may predispose to compression at the fibular head).\n- **Etiological Screening**: History of peripheral vascular disease, diabetes, or previous nerve injuries.")
B.aNh=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Neurological (Motor)**: \n    - **Strength**: Right ankle dorsiflexion (Tibialis Anterior) 3/5; Great toe extension (EHL) 3/5; Foot eversion (Peroneals) 4/5.\n    - **Localization Check**: **Tibialis Posterior (Inversion)** strength is 5/5 bilaterally. Hamstring strength (specifically common peroneal-innervated Biceps Femoris short head) is 5/5.\n- **Neurological (Sensory)**: Diminished light touch and pinprick sensation over the lateral leg and the dorsal aspect of the right foot. Sensation in the first dorsal webspace is significantly impaired.\n- **Reflexes**: Patellar and Achilles reflexes are 2+ and symmetric bilaterally. No Upper Motor Neuron (UMN) signs noted.\n- **MSK/Palpation**: Positive Tinel\u2019s sign at the right fibular head. A firm, minimally mobile mass is palpated posterior to the fibular neck.")
B.aPC=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical finding of foot drop with preserved inversion (Tibialis Posterior) and symmetric reflexes effectively localizes the lesion distal to the L5 nerve root and the lumbosacral plexus, pointing toward a common fibular (peroneal) neuropathy.")
B.aNi=new A.k(u.u,"1.  **Common Fibular Neuropathy at the Fibular Head**: Likely secondary to external compression or an internal mass (e.g., ganglion cyst).\n2.  **L5 Radiculopathy**: Though less likely given the preserved inversion and normal reflexes.\n3.  **Lumbosacral Plexopathy**: Specifically involving the lateral cord.\n4.  **Sciatic Neuropathy**: Involving the common fibular division (e.g., at the level of the sciatic notch or piriformis).\n5.  **Anterior Compartment Syndrome**: Although usually associated with severe pain and metabolic compromise.")
B.aQ8=new A.k("+ Formulate the appropriate diagnostic workup for this acute presentation.","1.  **Imaging**: **Duplex Ultrasound of the Fibular Head** to evaluate for structural compression. MRI of the right knee/leg may be indicated for surgical planning.\n2.  **Electrodiagnostic (EDX) Studies**: Though performable now to establish a baseline, the definitive utility for axonal loss (fibrillations) requires waiting **21 days post-injury**. Early NCS may show **conduction block** or slowed velocity across the fibular head.\n3.  **Radiography**: AP/Lateral X-rays of the knee to rule out bony exostosis or fractures.")
B.aQx=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Ultrasound reveals a 2.5cm anechoic, multiloculated mass communicating with the superior tibiofibular joint, effacing the common fibular nerve. This is consistent with an intraneural ganglion cyst.")
B.aOJ=new A.k("+ Describe the definitive management and prognostic counseling for this condition.","1.  **Decompression**: Prompt **ultrasound-guided aspiration** or surgical excision is indicated to relieve mechanical pressure. Many clinicians advocate for corticosteroid injection into the cyst cavity post-aspiration to reduce recurrence.\n2.  **Functional Support**: Provision of a **Solid or Leaf-Spring Ankle-Foot Orthosis (AFO)** to stabilize the gait and prevent falls secondary to foot-clearance deficits.\n3.  **Prognostic Counseling**: If the mechanism is purely compressive (**Neuropraxia**), recovery is often rapid (weeks). However, if axonal damage has occurred, recovery depends on regeneration at a rate of approximately **1 inch (1-2mm) per month**. Comprehensive recovery may take 6\u201312 months.")
B.aLz=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You are paged on a Friday afternoon. Your clinic's ultrasound machine is undergoing maintenance, and the local interventional radiology suite is closed for the weekend.")
B.aNY=new A.k("+ How do you demonstrate a 'Duty of Care' in coordinating this time-sensitive neurological emergency?","I would facilitate an immediate higher-level referral:\n1.  **Urgent Referral**: Coordinate with the on-call Neurosurgery or Orthopedic Surgery team at the nearest tertiary trauma center for emergency evaluation/decompression.\n2.  **Care Coordination**: Personally contact the receiving attending to relay the clinical urgency ('Time is Nerve') and the ultrasound findings of nerve effacement.\n3.  **Patient Safety**: Ensure the patient is provided with a temporary 'off-the-shelf' AFO or bracing to ensure safe ambulation until the procedure is performed.")
B.aOV=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: Two weeks after the procedure, the patient calls, sounding distressed: 'My foot is still weak and I have a huge bill from the ER. You said you'd fix me, but I'm no better and I'm out $2,000.'")
B.aLL=new A.k("+ Provide a professional response that manages expectations while validating the patient's experience.","I would respond: 'I hear your frustration, and I understand how discouraging it is to still be experiencing weakness after the procedure. To address the healing process: while we have successfully removed the 'source' of the pressure (the cyst), the nerve itself requires time to biologicaly recover from the injury it sustained. Think of it like a kink in a hose\u2014even after you straighten the hose, it takes time for the water to reach the end. Biological nerve regrowth is a slow process, moving at only about one inch per month. Our immediate priority is to ensure you can walk safely using the AFO we provided while we monitor for signals of nerve recovery. I want to see you in 2 weeks for a repeat physical exam and potentially schedule a detailed nerve test (EMG) to track that progress objectively.'")
B.apN=s([B.aKe,B.aQA,B.aNh,B.aPC,B.aNi,B.aQ8,B.aQx,B.aOJ,B.aLz,B.aNY,B.aOV,B.aLL],t.E)
B.PP=new A.bk("footdrop","Common Fibular Neuropathy secondary to Intraneural Ganglion Cyst",B.apN,!1)
B.aKK=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 22-year-old female presents to your clinic with persistent headaches and dizziness following a sports-related injury. Two weeks ago, she sustained an acceleration-deceleration injury (fall from a cheerleading stunt) striking her occiput against a gymnasium floor. She reports no loss of consciousness (GCS 15 at scene) but has had non-remitting symptoms since the event.")
B.aNT=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating this post-traumatic presentation, your history should focus on the four symptom clusters of concussion (SCAT6 framework):\n- **Somatic**: Characteristics of the headache (unilateral/pulsating vs. tension-type), photophobia, phonophobia, and nausea.\n- **Vestibular/Ocular**: Dizziness, vertigo, or 'foggy' sensation with rapid head movements.\n- **Cognitive**: Difficulty concentrating, memory deficits ('feeling slowed down').\n- **Sleep/Mood**: New-onset insomnia, emotional lability, or anxiety.\n- **Red Flag Screening**: Worsening headache, repeated vomiting, or focal neurologic signs that would suggest intracranial pathology.")
B.aOM=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vital Signs**: Stable. BP 115/75, HR 72.\n- **Cervical Spine**: Mild paraspinal tenderness and restricted ROM, ruling out significant cervicogenic contribution.\n- **Vestibular/Ocular-Motor Screening (VOMS)**: \n    - **Smooth Pursuit/Saccades**: Elicits mild headache.\n    - **Vestibular Ocular Reflex (VOR)**: Significant provocation of dizziness and nausea.\n    - **Visual Motion Sensitivity (VMS)**: Highly symptomatic.\n- **Balance**: Balance Error Scoring System (BESS) reveals deficits in single-leg stance on foam.\n- **Neurologic**: Cranial nerves II-XII are grossly intact. No focal motor or sensory deficits.")
B.aNa=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient demonstrates a symptom complex consistent with **mild Traumatic Brain Injury (concussion)** with predominant vestibular-ocular dysfunction and post-traumatic migraine phenotype.")
B.aKk=new A.k("+ Challenge: Explain the underlying pathophysiology of the 'Metabolic Crisis' in acute concussion/mTBI.","Concussion results in a biophysical force-induced neuronal depolarization. This triggers a massive release of glutamate and a subsequent influx of calcium and efflux of potassium. To restore homeostasis, the Na+/K+ pumps operate at maximum capacity, creating a state of **hyper-glycolysis** (increased energy demand). This occurs simultaneously with a period of **cerebral blood flow reduction** (decreased supply), leading to a 'mismatch' or metabolic crisis. During this vulnerable window, the brain is hypersensitive to secondary ischemic or traumatic injury.")
B.aPb=new A.k("+ What is your management strategy for her persistent vestibular symptoms and headaches?","1.  **Activity Modification**: Avoidance of 'contact risk' is mandatory. We no longer recommend 'cocoon therapy' (complete dark room rest). Instead, she should engage in **symptom-limited activity** (sub-threshold) to promote autonomic recovery.\n2.  **Vestibular Rehabilitation**: Referral to PT for a specialized vestibular program (e.g., gaze stability, habituation exercises).\n3.  **Headache Management**: \n    - **Abortive**: Limit NSAID/Tylenol use to prevent Medication Overuse Headache (MOH).\n    - **Prophylactic**: Given the 'migrainous' features (throbbing, photo/phonophobia), consider a trial of **Amitriptyline** (TCA) or **Topiramate** if symptoms persist beyond 4-6 weeks.")
B.aPl=new A.k("+ Would you obtain advanced neuroimaging (CT/MRI) at this visit?","According to the **ACR Appropriateness Criteria** and clinical decision rules (e.g., Canadian CT Head Rule), imaging is **not indicated** at this time. She is GCS 15, >24 hours post-injury, has no focal neurologic deficits, no signs of basilar skull fracture, and no coagulopathy. CT imaging is utilized to detect neurosurgical emergencies (hemorrhage), not diffuse axonal injury.")
B.aMg=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You note an increasing number of concussions in the local high school district without standardized baseline data.")
B.aNB=new A.k("+ How would you implement a 'Secondary Prevention' program for student-athletes?","I would propose the district-wide implementation of **Baseline Neurocognitive Testing** (e.g., ImPACT). This 'Systems-Based' initiative involves:\n1.  **Pre-season Screening**: Establishing a valid cognitive baseline for every contact-sport athlete.\n2.  **Post-injury Comparison**: Using objective data to assist in the difficult 'return-to-learn' and 'return-to-play' decision-making process, ensuring athletes have returned to their personal baseline before risk exposure.")
B.aNj=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient is symptom-free at rest but dizzy with exercise. She says: 'My coach says I'm fine if I don't have a headache. I have the State Championship in 4 days. I need you to clear me.'")
B.aPt=new A.k("+ Provide a professional response outlining the 'Graduated Return-to-Play' protocol.","I would respond: 'I understand how huge this championship is for you, and I want to get you back as soon as safely possible. However, the international definition of 'recovered' means you must be symptom-free *during physical exertion*, not just at rest. If you return before your brain's energy levels are restored, a second hit could be catastrophic (Second Impact Syndrome). We must follow the **6-Step Graduated Return-to-Play Protocol**: (1) Symptom-limited activity, (2) Light aerobic exercise, (3) Sport-specific exercise, (4) Non-contact drills, (5) Full-contact practice, and (6) Game play. Each step takes 24 hours. Since you become dizzy with exercise (Step 2), we cannot rush to Step 6 in four days. My medical clearance is based on your safety, and we have to follow the physiology.'")
B.aBn=s([B.aKK,B.aNT,B.aOM,B.aNa,B.aKk,B.aPb,B.aPl,B.aMg,B.aNB,B.aNj,B.aPt],t.E)
B.PO=new A.bk("headache","Post-Traumatic Headache and Vestibular-Ocular Dysfunction",B.aBn,!1)
B.aOr=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 50-year-old female presents to your outpatient clinic evaluating a 6-month history of progressive, daily headaches. She describes the pain as bilateral and dull, but notes it is distinctly worse in the mornings and wakes her from sleep.")
B.aN6=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating chronic daily headaches, your history should screen for **Secondary Headache Red Flags (SNOOP10)**:\n- **Systemic Symptoms**: Fever, weight loss (suggesting malignancy or infection).\n- **Neurologic Signs**: Confusion, impaired alertness, or focal deficits.\n- **Onset**: Sudden onset ('Thunderclap') vs. progressive.\n- **Older Age**: New onset >50 years old.\n- **Pattern Change**: Significant change in existing headache phenotype or **Positional** worsening (e.g., worse when lying flat/sleeping, suggestive of increased intracranial pressure).\n- **Precipitating Factors**: Worsening with Valsalva (coughing, sneezing).")
B.aPK=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vital Signs**: Stable. BP 138/84.\n- **Cervical Spine**: Mild tenderness to palpation of the cervical paraspinals; however, ROM is preserved.\n- **Neurologic (Cranial Nerves)**: \n    - **Hearing**: The patient consistently turns her head to the left to hear you; she admits to subjective hearing loss in the right ear.\n    - **Facial Sensation**: Mildly diminished sensation to light touch in the right V1/V2 distribution (Trigeminal nerve).\n- **Balance**: Check for cerebellar signs (e.g., dysdiadochokinesia) or gait ataxia, given the hearing loss (CN VIII) involvement.")
B.aMv=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient returns 2 months after a trial of conservative therapy for 'cervicogenic headaches.' Her symptoms have worsened. The clinical constellation of morning headaches, right-sided hearing loss (CN VIII), and facial numbness (CN V) localizes to the **Cerebellopontine Angle (CPA)**.")
B.aPL=new A.k("+ Based on this progression and the 'SNOOP10' criteria, what is your next diagnostic step?","I would order a **Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the Brain and Internal Auditory Canals**. The presence of 'Red Flags' (Age 50, progressive pattern, morning worsening) and focal cranial nerve deficits (CN V, VIII) necessitates neuroimaging to rule out an occupying mass lesion such as a Meningioma or Vestibular Schwannoma.")
B.aNg=new A.k("+ The MRI reveals an enhancing extra-axial mass in the right CPA consistent with a Meningioma. Describe your immediate management.","1.  **Disclosure**: Frame the diagnosis carefully, emphasizing that many meningiomas are benign (WHO Grade I) but require intervention due to their location.\n2.  **Referral**: Urgent consultation with **Neurosurgery** and **Radiation Oncology**.\n3.  **Steroids**: Initiation of **Dexamethasone** (with PPI prophylaxis) to reduce peritumoral edema if significant mass effect is present.")
B.aPu=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Following resection, the patient has moderate ataxia and requires assistance for ADLs. You recommend Acute Inpatient Rehabilitation (ARU), but the Medical Director denies admission, citing 'Lack of Medical Complexity'.")
B.aMu=new A.k("+ Construct an argument for why a Skilled Nursing Facility (SNF) may be the appropriate level of care under CMS guidelines.","Under CMS guidelines, Inpatient Rehabilitation Facilities (IRF/ARU) are reserved for patients requiring:\n1.  **Intensive Therapy**: Tolerance of 3 hours/day of therapy.\n2.  **Close Medical Supervision**: Daily face-to-face physician management for complex medical issues (e.g., unstable vitals, complex wound care, active titration of multiple medications).\n\nIf this patient is medically stable (post-operative, afebrile, stable meds) and her primary barrier is functional (ataxia), she may not meet the 'Medical Necessity' criteria for an ARU. A SNF can provide the necessary physical and occupational therapy at a lower intensity without the requirement for daily physician intervention.")
B.aOn=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: During a Telehealth follow-up at the SNF, you observe the patient in a soiled gown with a cluttered, dirty room. She states: 'I haven't seen a nurse in 6 hours. I can't get to the bathroom.'")
B.aLi=new A.k("+ Describe your obligations and actions as a Mandated Reporter.","As a physician, I am a **Mandated Reporter** for vulnerable adult abuse and neglect. My response would be:\n1.  **Immediate Safety**: Ask: 'Do you feel safe right now? Can you reach your call light?' If there is imminent danger, I would call 911.\n2.  **Facility Escalation**: Immediately contact the **Director of Nursing** and the **Facility Administrator** to report the specific conditions (soiled gown, lack of response) and demand an immediate welfare check.\n3.  **External Reporting**: I would file a formal report with the state's **Department of Public Health (DPH)** and contact the **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** to investigate the facility's failure to provide standard care. Neglect is not a 'customer service' issue; it is a reportable safety violation.")
B.aDH=s([B.aOr,B.aN6,B.aPK,B.aMv,B.aPL,B.aNg,B.aPu,B.aMu,B.aOn,B.aLi],t.E)
B.Pu=new A.bk("headaches","Intracranial Mass Lesion and Cancer Rehabilitation",B.aDH,!1)
B.aOt=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 16-year-old female soccer player presents with a 2-week history of progressive left posterior heel pain. She is a 'travel team' athlete and reports the pain limits her ability to sprint and push off.")
B.aPj=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating adolescent posterior heel pain, your history should focus on:\n- **Training Load**: Recent spikes in volume or intensity ('Load Capacity Mismatch').\n- **Pain Characterization**: Morning stiffness vs. warm-up phenomenon vs. pain after activity.\n- **Extrinsic Factors**: Footwear changes (cleats vs. running shoes), playing surface (turf vs. grass).\n- **Intrinsic Factors**: History of pes planus/cavus, prior injuries.\n- **Red Flags**: Night pain, constitutional symptoms, or history of flouroquinolone use.")
B.aPs=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Mild fusiform swelling noted 3cm proximal to the calcaneal insertion. No erythema or warmth.\n- **Palpation**: Tenderness to palpation in the mid-substance of the Achilles tendon. 'Arc sign' is present (nodule moves with tendon excursion).\n- **Range of Motion**: Ankle dorsiflexion is limited to 0 degrees with knee extended (Gastrocnemius tightness).\n- **Functional Testing**: \n    - **Single Leg Heel Raise**: Reproduction of pain and inability to complete >10 repetitions on the left (vs 25 on right).\n    - **Thompson Test**: Negative (intact tendon continuity).\n- **Special Tests**: Negative Talar Tilt and Anterior Drawer.")
B.aMU=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical presentation of mid-substance tenderness, morning stiffness, and pain with tendon loading is consistent with **Mid-portion Achilles Tendinopathy**.")
B.aNo=new A.k("+ Provide a structured differential diagnosis for posterior heel pain in this demographic.","1.  **Mid-portion Achilles Tendinopathy**: The primary working diagnosis.\n2.  **Retrocalcaneal Bursitis**: Inflammation of the bursa between the tendon and calcaneus.\n3.  **Haglund's Deformity**: 'Pump bump' or bony prominence irritating the insertion.\n4.  **Sever's Disease (Calcaneal Apophysitis)**: Though typically seen in younger adolescents (skeletally immature), it remains a consideration if growth plates are open.\n5.  **Posterior Ankle Impingement**: Os trigonum syndrome (common in dancers/soccer due to plantarflexion).\n6.  **Sural Neuritis**: Numbness/burning along the lateral border of the foot.")
B.aNu=new A.k("+ What is the preferred outcome measure to track her progress?","**The VISA-A Questionnaire** (Victorian Institute of Sport Assessment-Achilles). It is the validated gold-standard tool for assessing symptom severity and functional disability in Achilles tendinopathy.")
B.aLx=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You initiate a comprehensive rehabilitation program focusing on tendon mechanotransduction.")
B.aMj=new A.k("+ Describe the 'Gold Standard' exercise protocol for this condition.","The **Alfredson Protocol** (Eccentric Loading): \n- Investigates the use of heavy eccentric calf lowering (heel drops) to stimulate tendon remodeling.\n- **Protocol**: 3 sets of 15 repetitions, performed twice daily, for 12 weeks. \n- **Progression**: Performed with both straight knee (Gastrocnemius) and bent knee (Soleus). Load is added via a backpack or weights once bodyweight becomes pain-free.\n*Note*: Modern research also supports 'Heavy Slow Resistance' (HSR) training as an equivalent alternative.")
B.aQ7=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's coach contacts your office, stating: 'She's our leading scorer. If you don't clear her, we lose the playoffs. Can't you just inject it so she can play?'")
B.aN8=new A.k("+ How do you respond to this request regarding corticosteroid injection?","I would firmly advise **against** peritendinous corticosteroid injection in an active athlete. While it may provide short-term analgesic relief, high-quality evidence suggests it increases the risk of **tendon rupture** and inhibits long-term collagen healing. As a physician, my primary duty is to the long-term health of the patient's musculoskeletal system, not the short-term outcome of a game. Rupturing an Achilles would result in surgery and a 9-12 month recovery, which is an unacceptable risk.")
B.aNF=new A.k(u.j,"During a follow-up visit, the patient's mother steps out to take a call. The patient immediately reveals: 'My ankle barely hurts anymore. I just told you it hurts because I hate soccer. My parents force me to play year-round and I want to quit, but I'm afraid to tell them.'")
B.aOj=new A.k("+ Manage this ethical dilemma regarding patient autonomy and family dynamics.","I would respond: 'Thank you for trusting me enough to tell me the truth. I appreciate your honesty. It sounds like you are feeling incredibly pressured and burnt out, to the point where having an injury felt like the only way out. As your doctor, I care about your whole well-being\u2014mental and physical. Forcing yourself to play a sport you resent can actually lead to injury due to distraction or fatigue. I would like to help you have this conversation with your parents. We can frame it around your 'health goals' and the need to prevent burnout. Would you be open to me facilitating a discussion with them when they come back in, or would you prefer guidance on how to talk to them yourself later?'\n\n**Key Board Points**: Validate the patient's feelings, identify the 'Burnout' syndrome, and offer to act as a neutral advocate to facilitate family communication.")
B.aBd=s([B.aOt,B.aPj,B.aPs,B.aMU,B.aNo,B.aNu,B.aLx,B.aMj,B.aQ7,B.aN8,B.aNF,B.aOj],t.E)
B.PM=new A.bk("leftanklepain","Posterior Heel Pain in the Adolescent Athlete",B.aBd,!1)
B.aQq=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 27-year-old female presents with chronic, intractable left hand pain. Two years ago, she sustained a left scaphoid fracture (treated with ORIF) after dropping a weight. Although the fracture has healed, she reports progressive burning pain, swelling, and extreme sensitivity to touch that has prevented her from returning to work as a chef.")
B.aMV=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating suspected neuropathic pain syndromes, your history should screen for the four symptom categories of the **Budapest Criteria**:\n- **Sensory**: Hyperesthesia (increased sensitivity) and Allodynia (pain to non-painful stimuli).\n- **Vasomotor**: Temperature asymmetry (hot/cold) and skin color changes.\n- **Sudomotor/Edema**: Asymmetric sweating or edema.\n- **Motor/Trophic**: Decreased range of motion, weakness, or trophic changes (hair/nail growth abnormalities).\n- **Etiology**: Differentiating Type I (no nerve lesion) vs. Type II (known nerve lesion).")
B.aPv=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: The left hand is erythematous and edematous compared to the right. There is glossy skin texture and coarse hair growth on the dorsum of the hand.\n- **Palpation**: Significant **mechanical allodynia**; the patient withdraws from light touch (cotton swab).\n- **Temperature**: The left hand is palpably cooler than the right (Vasomotor instability).\n- **Range of Motion**: Global restriction in finger flexion/extension due to pain and edema.\n- **Neurologic**: No specific dermatomal sensory deficit (ruling out radiculopathy or peripheral nerve entrapment).")
B.aPq=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical presentation meets the **Budapest Clinical Criteria** for Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) Type I (formerly RSD).")
B.aM_=new A.k("+ What diagnostic studies would support this diagnosis and rule out mimics?","1.  **Triple Phase Bone Scan**: May show diffuse uptake in the delayed (mineralization) phase in the affected carpal bones.\n2.  **Plain Radiographs**: Evaluation for patchy osteopenia (**Sudeck's Atrophy**).\n3.  **Electrodiagnostic Studies (EMG/NCS)**: To rule out CRPS Type II (e.g., Median or Radial neuropathy) or cervical radiculopathy.\n4.  **Inflammatory Markers (ESR/CRP)**: To rule out infectious or rheumatologic processes.")
B.aMX=new A.k("+ Describe your comprehensive management plan for this patient.","1.  **Pharmacologic**: 'Neuropathic Ladder' including Gabapentin/Pregabalin, TCAs (Amitriptyline), or SNRIs (Duloxetine). Topical Lidocaine/Capsaicin for allodynia.\n2.  **Rehabilitation (The Cornerstone)**: \n    - **Desensitization**: Texture rubbing protocols.\n    - **Graded Motor Imagery (GMI)**: A sequential program of (1) Laterality Reconstruction (Left/Right discrimination), (2) Motor Imagery, and (3) Mirror Therapy to re-map the cortical representation.\n    - **Edema Control**: Compression garments and active ROM.\n3.  **Psychological**: CBT for coping mechanisms and pain catastrophizing.")
B.aOg=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient fails conservative management and pharmacotherapy. Her pain remains 8/10.")
B.aPc=new A.k("+ What is the next step in the 'Interventional Ladder'?","I would refer for a **Sympathetic Nerve Block** (Stellate Ganglion Block for the upper extremity). This serves both diagnostic (is the pain sympathetically maintained?) and therapeutic purposes. \n\n*Clinical Clue*: If she responds well but pain returns, she may be a candidate for **Neuromodulation** (Spinal Cord Stimulation or Dorsal Root Ganglion Stimulation).")
B.aOq=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient returns, frustrated. She states: 'I can't work. I'm chef and I can't even hold a knife. I need you to sign this form declaring me **permanently disabled** so I can get my payout.'")
B.aPA=new A.k("+ How do you manage this request for permanent disability?","I would explain the concept of **Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI)**. \nresponse: 'I completely agree that *currently* you are unable to work as a chef due to your pain capability. I am happy to write for **temporary total disability** to support you while we treat this. However, we have not yet exhausted our treatment options (e.g., Neuromodulation). Declaring 'permanent' disability means we imply you will *never* get better, and I still have hope for your recovery. Let's focus on the next treatment step to get function back, rather than closing the door on your career today.'")
B.aQu=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient becomes enraged at your refusal to sign for permanent disability. She screams: 'You're useless! You're just trying to save the insurance company money! I'm not leaving until you sign this!'")
B.aLP=new A.k("+ Demonstrate how to de-escalate this hostile encounter.","I would maintain a calm, non-confrontational posture and sit down (if safe) to lower the energy. \nResponse: 'I can see how angry and desperate you are, and I want you to know I am on your team, not the insurance company's. My refusal isn't about money; it's about my medical belief that you still have potential to heal. Non-maleficence means I cannot sign a document I believe is medically inaccurate. \n\nHowever, if you feel our relationship is fractured, I respect your right to a second opinion. I cannot sign this form today. If you are refusing to leave, I will have to ask security to escort you out for the safety of the clinic, but I would prefer we end this visit calmly. I will provide you with a referral to a colleague for a fresh perspective.'")
B.aCf=s([B.aQq,B.aMV,B.aPv,B.aPq,B.aM_,B.aMX,B.aOg,B.aPc,B.aOq,B.aPA,B.aQu,B.aLP],t.E)
B.Pz=new A.bk("lefthandpain","Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS) Type I",B.aCf,!1)
B.aME=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 42-year-old male presents with a 4-week history of subacute low back pain radiating into the left lateral thigh and calf. He denies any inciting trauma.")
B.aKV=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating lumbar radicular pain, your history should focus on:\n- **Dermatomal Distribution**: Does the pain radiate past the knee? (L5 radiating to the dorsum of the foot vs. L4 to the medial malleolus).\n- **Red Flag Screening (Cauda Equina)**: Bowel/bladder incontinence, saddle anesthesia, or progressive bilateral weakness.\n- **Positional Factors**: Flexion intolerance (Discogenic) vs. Extension intolerance (Stenosis/Facet).\n- **Systemic Signs**: History of cancer, unremitting night pain, or fever.\n- **Functional Impact**: Difficulty with heel walking (L5) vs. toe walking (S1).")
B.aKa=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Gait**: Antalgic. Unable to perform left heel-walk.\n- **Neurologic (Motor)**: \n    - **L5**: Tibialis Anterior (Dorsiflexion) 4/5; Extensor Hallucis Longus (Gt Toe Ext) 4/5.\n    - **Localization Key**: **Gluteus Medius (Hip Abduction)** is 4-/5. Posterior Tibialis (Inversion) is 5/5.\n- **Neurologic (Sensory)**: Diminished sensation in the L5 dermatome (lateral calf and first dorsal webspace).\n- **Provocative Maneuvers**: \n    - **Straight Leg Raise**: Positive on the left at 45 degrees.\n    - **FABER/FADIR**: Negative for hip pathology.\n- **Non-Organic Signs**: Negative Waddell's signs.")
B.aLW=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture of dermatomal pain radiating below the knee with myotomal weakness in L5-innervated muscles (Tibialis Anterior, EHL, Gluteus Medius) is consistent with an **L5 Radiculopathy**.")
B.aQp=new A.k("+ Explain the significance of the Gluteus Medius weakness in your differential diagnosis.","Gluteus Medius weakness (Trendelenburg sign) is the **key differentiator** between an **L5 Radiculopathy** and a **Common Peroneal Neuropathy**. \n- The Gluteus Medius is innervated by the **Superior Gluteal Nerve**, which arises from the L5/S1 nerve roots *proximal* to the sciatic/peroneal division. \n- A peroneal nerve injury at the knee would cause foot drop but would *spare* the hip abductors. Its involvement here confirms the lesion is at the root/plexus level.")
B.aQ3=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient has 4-/5 weakness (not paralyzed) and manageable pain. He asks for an MRI.")
B.aLX=new A.k("+ Describe your management strategy regarding imaging and treatment.","1.  **Imaging (The 6-Week Rule)**: In the absence of 'Red Flags' (Cauda Equina, profound/progressive weakness, infection), MRI is **not indicated** in the first 6 weeks. The natural history of lumbar disc herniation is favorable, with 90% resolving with conservative care.\n2.  **Conservative Care**: Initiate a 6-week course of physical therapy (directional preference/McKenzie method), NSAIDs, and activity modification.\n3.  **Progression**: If symptoms persist beyond 6 weeks or neurologic deficits worsen, MRI and/or Epidural Steroid Injections (ESI) become indicated.")
B.aPQ=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Six weeks later, the patient has not improved. You order an EMG/NCS before the MRI. The patient is upset: 'Why am I paying for a nerve test? Just scan my back!'")
B.aNI=new A.k("+ Justify the medical necessity of Electrodiagnostics (EMG) prior to or alongside MRI.","I would explain: 'MRI is an **anatomic** test\u2014it shows us what the spine *looks* like. However, many people have disc bulges that are completely painless (false positives). The EMG is a **physiologic** test\u2014it tells us how the nerves are *function*. \n- By doing the EMG, we confirm that the disc bulge we see on MRI is actually the one causing your weakness.\n- Furthermore, it rules out mimic conditions (like a pinched nerve at the knee) that an MRI of the back would miss completely. This ensures we don't operate on the wrong level.'")
B.aLV=new A.k(u.j,"The EMG confirms active L5 denervation. The patient demands surgery immediately. 'I'm done with therapy. Cut it out.' However, the surgeon deems him a poor candidate due to morbid obesity and uncontrolled diabetes (A1c 11.0).")
B.aK6=new A.k("+ How do you counsel the patient regarding surgical risk optimization?","I would respond: 'I completely understand your desire for a quick fix, but surgery right now carries extreme risks. With an A1c of 11.0, your risk of a **post-operative wound infection** or failed fusion is exceptionally high. No ethical surgeon will operate until your body is safe to heal. This isn't a 'no' forever; it's a 'not yet.' We need to use this time to aggressively manage your diabetes and continue non-operative pain management (like injections) so that if you *do* eventually need surgery, you will survive it and have a successful outcome.'")
B.aDO=s([B.aME,B.aKV,B.aKa,B.aLW,B.aQp,B.aQ3,B.aLX,B.aPQ,B.aNI,B.aLV,B.aK6],t.E)
B.PV=new A.bk("leftlowerextremitypain","Subacute Lumbar Radiculopathy",B.aDO,!1)
B.aMb=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 45-year-old male software engineer presents with a 1-month history of progressive left shoulder pain, primarily located in the anterolateral aspect. He reports a 'catching' sensation when reaching overhead.")
B.aQn=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating subacute shoulder pain, your history should delineate:\n- **Pain Characterization**: Night pain (suggesting rotator cuff pathology) vs. stiffness (suggesting adhesive capsulitis).\n- **Mechanism**: Insidious onset vs. acute trauma.\n- **Occupational Ergonomics**: Prolonged computer use (protracted shoulders/cervical kyphosis).\n- **Cervical Screen**: Rule out C5-C6 radiculopathy referring to the deltoid region.\n- **Diabetes Screen**: Important risk factor for Adhesive Capsulitis.")
B.aO2=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Note **Scapular Dyskinesis** (SICK Scapula syndrome: Scapular malposition, Inferior medial border prominence, Coracoid pain, and dysKinesis of movement).\n- **Range of Motion**: Painful Arc between 60-120 degrees of abduction. Passive ER/IR is preserved (ruling out Adhesive Capsulitis).\n- **Impingement Tests**: Positive Neer's and Hawkins-Kennedy signs.\n- **AC Joint**: Negative Cross-Body Adduction.\n- **Labrum**: Negative O'Brien's Test (Active Compression Test).\n- **Neurologic**: Normal strength in C5-T1 myotomes. Sensation intact.")
B.aQ_=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture of a painful arc, preserved passive ROM, and positive impingement signs is consistent with **Subacromial Impingement Syndrome (SAIS)**.")
B.aMZ=new A.k("+ Describe the Neer Classification of Impingement that corresponds to the pathophysiology here.","1.  **Stage I**: Edema and hemorrhage (typically <25 years old).\n2.  **Stage II**: Fibrosis and tendonitis (25-40 years old).\n3.  **Stage III**: Bone spur formation and tendon rupture (>40 years old).")
B.aMn=new A.k("+ Imaging Update: X-rays reveal a discrete, heterogeneous calcific density within the supraspinatus tendon. MRI confirms 'Calcific Tendonitis' without full-thickness tear.","This confirms the diagnosis of **Calcific Tendonitis**. The calcific deposit occupies space in the subacromial outlet, mechanically worsening the impingement.")
B.aLv=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient has failed 6 weeks of physical therapy and NSAIDs. He is unable to sleep due to pain.")
B.aOx=new A.k("+ Describe an Ultrasound-Guided procedure that specifically targets this pathology.","I would recommend **Ultrasound-Guided Barbotage (Lavage)**.\n- **Technique**: Under sterile ultrasound guidance, an 18-22G needle is advanced into the calcific deposit. Saline and local anesthetic are pulsed to 'wash out' (lavage) the calcium crystals. The tendon is then fenestrated to stimulate healing, followed by a subacromial corticosteroid injection for post-procedural pain control.\n- **Rationale**: This addresses the *root cause* (calcium volume) rather than just masking the pain.")
B.aOT=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient has a history of Type 2 Diabetes. You plan to proceed with the steroid injection component.")
B.aOp=new A.k("+ How do you counsel him regarding the systemic effects of the injection?","I would counsel him on the **'300 Rule'** and transient hyperglycemia:\n- Corticosteroids can transiently elevate blood glucose for 3-7 days post-injection.\n- He should check his blood sugars frequently (q4h) for the next 48 hours.\n- If blood sugar persistently exceeds >300 mg/dL, he should contact his Primary Care Provider or Endocrinologist for temporary insulin adjustment to prevent DKA/HHS.")
B.aPM=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient's brother (a Physical Therapist) calls you aggressively: 'Why are you doing a lavage? The evidence says surgery is the only way to fix a tear! You're wasting his time.'")
B.aNC=new A.k("+ Provide an Evidence-Based response to this professional challenge.","I would respond: 'I appreciate your advocacy for your brother, and I respect your clinical expertise. Regarding the evidence, the **MOON Shoulder Group** studies and other high-level RCTs have demonstrated that for atraumatic rotator cuff pathology, conservative management (including injections/PT) has equivalent 2-year outcomes to surgery, without the operative risks. Specifically for *Calcific Tendonitis*, Barbotage has a high success rate in resorbing the deposit. Surgery remains a valid option, but it is standard of care to attempt less invasive, effective measures first. I want to save him from a surgery if we can.'")
B.aDL=s([B.aMb,B.aQn,B.aO2,B.aQ_,B.aMZ,B.aMn,B.aLv,B.aOx,B.aOT,B.aOp,B.aPM,B.aNC],t.E)
B.PE=new A.bk("leftshoulderpain","Calcific Tendinopathy of the Shoulder",B.aDL,!1)
B.aLI=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 63-year-old male with a history of poorly controlled Type 2 Diabetes (A1c 8.3%) presents with subacute, excruciating left anterior thigh pain and a recent unintentional 30-lb weight loss.")
B.aOz=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating suspected diabetic neuropathy variants, your history should distinguish between:\n- **Symptom Profile**: Deep, burning, dysesthetic pain (Small fiber) vs. weakness/atrophy (Large fiber/Motor).\n- **Distribution**: Asymmetric, proximal involvement (DLRPN) vs. symmetric, distal, length-dependent (Standard Polyneuropathy).\n- **Constitutional**: Significant weight loss ('Diabetic Cachexia') is a hallmark of DLRPN.\n- **Autonomic**: Orthostasis, gastroparesis, or erectile dysfunction.\n- **Falls History**: Assessing for quadriceps buckling vs. proprioceptive loss.")
B.aP1=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Significant atrophy of the left quadriceps (Vastus Medialis/Lateralis) compared to the right.\n- **Motor**: \n    - Left Hip Flexion (L2/3): 3/5.\n    - Left Knee Extension (L3/4): 3/5.\n    - Left Hip Adduction (Obturator L2-4): 4-/5 (Key differentiator from pure Femoral Neuropathy).\n    - Distal strength is preserved.\n- **Sensory**: Allodynia over the anterior thigh; decreased pinprick in the saphenous distribution.\n- **Reflexes**: Absent Left Patellar reflex; Achilles reflex 1+.")
B.aMe=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The constellation of asymmetric proximal weakness, severe pain, weight loss, and involvement of multiple nerve territories (Femoral + Obturator) in a diabetic patient is diagnostic of **Diabetic Lumbosacral Radiculoplexus Neuropathy (DLRPN)**, historically known as Diabetic Amyotrophy.")
B.aLK=new A.k("+ Describe the pathophysiology of DLRPN compared to standard diabetic polyneuropathy.","Unlike the metabolic/toxic mechanism of standard polyneuropathy, DLRPN is believed to be an **immune-mediated ischemic microvasculitis**. Inflammation of the vasa nervorum leads to ischemic infarction of the lumbosacral plexus nerve roots.")
B.aNH=new A.k("+ What is your management strategy given this immune-mediated etiology?","1.  **Glycemic Control**: While critical for long-term health, rapid correction can sometimes worsen symptoms ('Insulin Neuritis'), so a graded approach is preferred.\n2.  **Pain Management**: First-line agents include Gabapentinoids (Pregabalin) or SNRIs (Duloxetine). Opioids may be required for the severe acute phase.\n3.  **Immunotherapy**: While corticosteroids or IVIG are theoretically sound given the ischemic/inflammatory mechanism, conclusive evidence for their efficacy in DLRPN is lacking, and steroids may exacerbate the diabetes. Conservative management is the standard of care.")
B.aMl=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Two months later, the patient is admitted for recurrent falls. He is found to have a catastrophic drop in hemoglobin (7.6 g/dL) and a CT reveals a massive **Left Retroperitoneal Hematoma** compressing the Femoral nerve.")
B.aLT=new A.k("+ You are consulted for Inpatient Rehabilitation. The medical director denies admission citing 'medical stability'. Appeal this decision.","I would argue that this patient has **Medical Complexity** requiring hospital-level Rehabilitation:\n1.  **Hemodynamic Monitoring**: He has an active retroperitoneal bleed requiring daily CBC monitoring and orthostatic vitals; a distinct risk for hypovolemic shock if he re-bleeds during therapy.\n2.  **Neurologic 'Double Crush'**: He now has a compressive femoral neuropathy superimposed on the DLRPN, rendering him a high fall risk (2/5 quad strength).\n3.  **Complex Nursing**: He requires insulin titration (sliding scale) in the setting of hematoma stress-response, which cannot be safely managed in a SNF.")
B.aMd=new A.k(u.j,"On the rehab unit, the patient is tearful and holds your hand. He says: 'Doctor, you've been a blessing. I'm scared about my future. Will you pray with me?'")
B.aNk=new A.k("+ How do you professionally navigate this request for spiritual engagement?","I would use the **FICA Spiritual History** framework (Faith, Importance, Community, Address in Care). \nResponse: 'I am honored that you feel comfortable sharing proper spiritual vulnerability with me. \n\n*Scenario A (You are comfortable)*: 'I would be happy to sit with you for a moment of silence or prayer to support your healing.'\n\n*Scenario B (You prefer not to)*: 'I have deep respect for your faith and how it supports your recovery. While I prefer to keep my professional role separate from prayer, I can certainly sit with you while you pray, or I would be delighted to call our **Hospital Chaplain** to join us right now so you can have that spiritual support.'")
B.aBx=s([B.aLI,B.aOz,B.aP1,B.aMe,B.aLK,B.aNH,B.aMl,B.aLT,B.aMd,B.aNk],t.E)
B.Pw=new A.bk("legpain","Diabetic Lumbosacral Radiculoplexus Neuropathy (DLRPN)",B.aBx,!1)
B.aQz=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 14-year-old female is currently on your Inpatient Rehabilitation unit recovering from a severe Traumatic Brain Injury (Subdural Hematoma s/p Craniectomy). Evaluation of her morning labs is pending. The nurse pages you stat: the patient is unusually lethargic and her heart rate has dropped to 50 bpm.")
B.aN1=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In assessing an acute decline in mental status in a TBI patient, you must rapidly screen for:\n- **Raised Intracranial Pressure (ICP)**: Headache, projectile vomiting, Cushing's Triad (Bradycardia, Hypertension, Irregular Respirations).\n- **Infectious**: Fever, nuchal rigidity (Meningitis), or wound drainage.\n- **Seizure**: Post-ictal state following an unwitnessed event.\n- **Metabolic**: Hypoglycemia or Hyponatremia (CSW/SIADH).\n- **Medication**: Recent administration of sedatives/opioids.")
B.aO5=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: HR 48 (Bradycardic), BP 160/90 (Hypertensive), RR 10 (Irregular). **(This is Cushing's Triad)**.\n- **Eyes**: Left pupil is 6mm and sluggish. Right is 3mm and reactive. 'Sunset Eye' sign (impaired upgaze) noted.\n- **Neurologic**: GCS has dropped from 14 (E4, V4, M6) to 9 (E2, V3, M4). She withdraws to pain but does not follow commands.\n- **Incision**: Clean, dry, and intact. No boggy swelling (pseudomeningocele).")
B.aO9=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The triad of Bradycardia, Hypertension, and Irregular Respirations (Cushing's Reflex) alongside pupillary asymmetry is diagnostic of **Acute Hydrocephalus** with impending herniation.")
B.aLl=new A.k("+ Explain the pathophysiology of the Cushing's Reflex.","Ischemia to the brainstem (due to high ICP) triggers a sympathetic surge to increase Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) and maintain cerebral perfusion. In response, the baroreceptors cause a parasympathetic reflex bradycardia via the Vagus nerve.")
B.aKt=new A.k("+ What is your immediate management?","1.  **Stat Head CT**: To confirm ventriculomegaly/hydrocephalus.\n2.  **Neurosurgery Consult**: Urgent evaluation for EVD (External Ventricular Drain) or VP Shunt (Ventriculoperitoneal Shunt) placement.\n3.  **Medical Management**: Elevate head of bed to 30 degrees. Hyperventilate (target pCO2 30-35 mmHg) for transient vasoconstriction if herniation is imminent.")
B.aP0=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","A VP shunt is placed. The patient stabilizes and returns to the unit. She enters **Rancho Los Amigos Level IV (Confused-Agitated)**: she is restless, hitting staff, and screaming.")
B.aLt=new A.k("+ Describe your environmental and pharmacologic management strategy for RLA IV Agitation.","1.  **Environmental**: 'Low Stimulation' protocol. Dim lights, minimize noise/visitors, use a Craig Bed (enclosed safety bed) or 1:1 sitter for safety. Avoid restraints if possible as they increase agitation.\n2.  **Pharmacologic**: \n    - **First line**: Environmental modification.\n    - **Medications**: Propranolol (Beta-blocker) or Amantadine. Avoid Benzodiazepines (worsen confusion/memory) and Antipsychotics (lower seizure threshold) unless absolutely necessary for safety.")
B.aOe=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The insurance company attempts to deny her continued inpatient stay, stating: 'She is too agitated to participate in 3 hours of therapy. Send her to a skilled nursing facility (SNF).'")
B.aNe=new A.k("+ Conduct the Peer-to-Peer appeal. Justify the medical necessity of Acute Rehab over SNF.","I would argue: 'This patient is in the **Rancho IV** stage of recovery, which is a critical window for brain injury rehabilitation. \n1.  **Safety**: Her agitation and confusion require a **locked unit** or **1:1 safety monitors** and specialized enclosure beds (Craig bed), which standard SNFs cannot provide. A SNF would likely chemically restrain her (sedation), which halts her cognitive recovery.\n2.  **Therapy Function**: Agitation *is* the therapy target. Our neuropsychologists and therapists are working solely on behavior modification and command following. We document participation by 'time engaged' rather than 'reps,' which meets CMS criteria for this population.'")
B.aOw=new A.k(u.j,"The patient's mother is upset with your 'Low Stimulation' rule restricting visitors. 'She needs her family! We are her support system! Why are you keeping us away?'")
B.aLg=new A.k("+ Counsel the mother on the concept of 'Overstimulation' in brain injury.","I would explain: 'I know this feels like we are isolating her, and that goes against every instinct you have as a mother. But right now, her brain is like a computer that is rebooting\u2014it is very fragile. Too much noise, talking, and touching causes her brain to 'overheat,' which comes out as screaming and hitting (agitation). By keeping the room quiet and limiting visitors to one at a time, we are actually conducting a medical treatment\u2014we are giving her brain the rest it needs to heal faster so she *can* recognize you and enjoy your company sooner. We are not keeping you away; we are pacing the visits to match what her brain can handle.'")
B.aD2=s([B.aQz,B.aN1,B.aO5,B.aO9,B.aLl,B.aKt,B.aP0,B.aLt,B.aOe,B.aNe,B.aOw,B.aLg],t.E)
B.Q2=new A.bk("lethargy","Pediatric Traumatic Brain Injury & Hydrocephalus",B.aD2,!1)
B.aKU=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 56-year-old male auto mechanic presents with a 1-year history of chronic low back pain and bilateral leg heaviness. He notes the pain is worse when standing or walking and improves when he sits down.")
B.aP3=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating chronic low back pain with radiation, accurately distinguish **Neurogenic** vs. **Vascular** Claudication:\n- **Positional**: Does leaning forward on a shopping cart relieve pain? (Positive 'Shopping Cart Sign' = Neurogenic/Stenosis).\n- **Distance vs. Effort**: Is it strictly distance-dependent regardless of posture? (Vascular).\n- **Uphill/Downhill**: Does walking downhill (extension) worsen it? (Neurogenic).\n- **Red Flags**: History of cancer, fever, IV drug use, or bowel/bladder incontinence.\n- **PMH**: Significant for Atrial Fibrillation with a Pacemaker.")
B.aMF=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Gait**: Wide-based, slightly stooped forward (Simian stance).\n- **Vascular**: Distal pulses 2+ and symmetric (Ruling out Vascular Claudication).\n- **Neurologic**: Normal strength and sensation. Reflexes 2+ throughout.\n- **Provocative Maneuvers**: \n    - **Extension Loading**: Pain reproduction with lumbar extension.\n    - **Straight Leg Raise**: Negative.\n    - **FABER**: Negative for hip pathology.")
B.aOU=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical presentation of extension-intolerance and relief with flexion is classic for **Lumbar Spinal Stenosis (Neurogenic Claudication)** secondary to Spondylosis.")
B.aKs=new A.k("+ You wish to order an MRI, but he has a Pacemaker. How do you proceed?","I would not simply assume it is contraindicated. I would obtain the **Make and Model** of the device to check for **MRI-Conditionality**. Most modern pacemakers have an 'MRI Mode.' \n- *Scenario A*: If Conditional, proceed with MRI Lumbar Spine (w/ cardiology clearance).\n- *Scenario B*: If Non-Compatible, order a **CT Myelogram**. This provides the necessary evaluation of thecal sac compression that a standard CT misses.")
B.aOs=new A.k("+ Imaging findings (CT Myelogram) reveal Severe Central Canal Stenosis at L4-5 due to Facet Hypertrophy.","Note: Severe stenosis is often defined by the **Schizas Classification** (Grade C/D: minimal to no fluid visible around nerve roots).")
B.aMH=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient has failed physical therapy and oral NSAIDs. He asks about 'burning the nerves' (RFA).")
B.aNE=new A.k("+ Describe the standard of care protocol for Radiofrequency Ablation (RFA).","One does not jump straight to RFA. The **Spine Intervention Society (SIS)** guidelines require:\n1.  **Diagnostic Block 1**: Medial Branch Block (MBB) with distinct anesthetic (e.g., Lidocaine).\n2.  **Diagnostic Block 2**: Confirmatory MBB with a different anesthetic (e.g., Bupivacaine) on a separate date.\n3.  **Threshold**: Both blocks must provide **>80% pain relief** for the duration of the anesthetic to qualify for Thermal RFA.")
B.aP2=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You refer him to Interventional Pain. Two months later, he returns stating the procedure didn't help. He asks: 'My wife gave me one of her hydrocodones and it's the only thing that works. Can you just write me a script for that?'")
B.aMc=new A.k("+ Manage this request for opioids and the misuse of diverted medication.","I would firmly but compassionately refuse.\nResponse: 'I understand you are in severe pain, but I cannot fulfill that request. First, taking medication prescribed for someone else is unsafe and illegal (diversion). Second, current **CDC Guidelines** do not support the use of long-term opioids for chronic non-cancer back pain, as the risks of dependence and hyperalgesia outweigh the benefits. I cannot start you on hydrocodone. However, I *can* offer multimodal alternatives: we can try duloxetine (Cymbalta) for the nerve pain, or refer you to a surgeon since conservative care has failed for your severe stenosis.'")
B.aOh=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient becomes angry. 'You doctors are all the same. You let me suffer because you're scared of the DEA! I can't work! Write me a disability letter then!'")
B.aMp=new A.k("+ How do you respond to the demand for disability paperwork in this context?","I would validate his frustration without conceding to the demand immediately. \nResponse: 'I hear your frustration, and I see how much this pain is affecting your livelihood. I am not refusing pills to protect myself, but to protect you from their long-term harm. regarding work: I can certainly write a note detailing your **functional restrictions** (e.g., no lifting >10lbs, sit/stand option). However, declaring 'permanent disability' is a legal determination that requires a specific **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)**. I am happy to refer you for an FCE so we have objective data to support your claim properly.'")
B.aBY=s([B.aKU,B.aP3,B.aMF,B.aOU,B.aKs,B.aOs,B.aMH,B.aNE,B.aP2,B.aMc,B.aOh,B.aMp],t.E)
B.Py=new A.bk("lowbackpain1","Lumbar Spondylosis & Neurogenic Claudication",B.aBY,!1)
B.aNb=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 73-year-old female with a history of hypertension and Coronary Artery Disease presents with acute, severe mid-back pain after a ground-level fall (slipping on a wet floor) 3 days ago.")
B.aNK=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating acute geriatric back pain after trauma, focus on:\n- **Mechanism**: Low-energy (fragility) vs. High-energy trauma.\n- **Neurologic Screen**: Any bowel/bladder incontinence, saddle anesthesia, or lower extremity weakness (retropulsed fragment compressing the cord/cauda equina).\n- **Red Flags**: Unrelenting night pain or fever (Malignancy/Discitis).\n- **Constitutional**: Recent weight loss or history of malignancy.\n- **Social**: Current living situation and fall risk at home.")
B.aKD=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Kyphotic sustained posture. No bruising or abrasions.\n- **Palpation**: Point tenderness at the T10-L1 thoracolumbar junction. Percussion tenderness is positive.\n- **Neurologic**: Strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities. Sensation and reflexes intact. Rectal tone normal.\n- **Functional**: Unable to transition from sit-to-stand without significant assistance due to pain.")
B.aQs=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The history of low-energy trauma in an elderly female with point tenderness is highly suspicious for a **Vertebral Compression Fracture (VCF)**.")
B.aOm=new A.k("+ Imaging Update: X-rays reveal a T12 Anterior Wedge Compression Fracture with 30% height loss. No retropulsion.","This is a **Genant Grade 2** (25-40% height loss) compression fracture. \n*Critical Step*: Verify stability by assessing the **Posterior Ligamentous Complex (PLC)**. Absence of widening between spinous processes on palpation/X-ray suggests a stable, anterior-column only injury.")
B.aN7=new A.k("+ Describe your comprehensive medical management plan.","1.  **Analgesia**: Multimodal approach (Acetaminophen, Lidocaine patch). **Calcitonin-Salmon (Intranasal)** is a unique adjunct shown to reduce acute VCF pain for 4 weeks.\n2.  **Bracing**: A **Posterior Inflatable (PKB)** or Jewett brace to prevent flexion and promote extension (comfort only, does not heal the bone).\n3.  **Mobilization**: Early mobilization as tolerated to prevent DVT/Pneumonia. Avoid bedrest.\n4.  **Secondary Prevention (CRITICAL)**: This is a fragility fracture. She needs a **DEXA scan** and initiation of anti-osteoporotic therapy (Bisphosphonates or Anabolics like Teriparatide) to prevent the next hip fracture.")
B.aK8=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Six weeks later, she returns with persistent, debilitating pain (VAS 8/10) despite bracing and meds. She cannot sleep or function.")
B.aQ6=new A.k("+ Discuss the role of Vertebroplasty/Kyphoplasty in this scenario.","This is controversial. \n- **AAOS Guidelines**: Recommend *against* routine use due to sham-controlled trials showing no long-term benefit over medical management.\n- **VAPOUR Trial (Lancet)**: Showed that in fractures <6 weeks old with severe pain, vertebroplasty provided significantly better pain relief than sham.\n- **Decision**: Given her intractable pain failing 6 weeks of conservative care, she is a candidate for **Vertebroplasty/Kyphoplasty** for *palliation*, understanding the risks (cement leakage, adjacent level fracture).")
B.aOK=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You refer her for the procedure. The Spine Surgeon calls you angrily: 'Why did you wait 6 weeks? You delayed her care! She needed this immediately!'")
B.aO7=new A.k("+ Role-play your professional defense of your conservative management.","I would respond: 'I appreciate your passion for her recovery. My decision to trial 6 weeks of conservative care aligns with the **AAOS Clinical Practice Guidelines**, which prioritize non-operative management due to the favorable natural history of most VCFs. Evidence suggests that early vertebroplasty does not always confer long-term benefit over placebo. However, now that she has failed that trial and meets the criteria for 'intractable pain,' I agree she is an excellent surgical candidate. I referred her to you because I trust your technical skills to help her now that conservative measures have been exhausted.'")
B.aBp=s([B.aNb,B.aNK,B.aKD,B.aQs,B.aOm,B.aN7,B.aK8,B.aQ6,B.aOK,B.aO7],t.E)
B.Q1=new A.bk("lowbackpain2","Osteoporotic Vertebral Compression Fracture",B.aBp,!1)
B.aPn=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 29-year-old female presents with a 3-year history of involuntary neck turning and chronic neck pain. She describes a 'pulling' sensation that forces her head to the right, which worsens with stress and improves with a 'sensory trick' (lightly touching her chin).")
B.aN5=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating involuntary movements, your history should distinguish:\n- **Dystonia Classification**: Focal (Cervical) vs. Segmental vs. Generalized.\n- **Sensory Geste**: Does touching the chin/face temporarily resolve the posture? (Classic for Dystonia).\n- **Medication History**: Any exposure to Dopamine antagonists (Antipsychotics/Reglan) causing Tardive Dystonia?\n- **Family History**: DYT1 gene mutations (Early Onset Dystonia).\n- **Red Flags**: Wilson's Disease screening (young onset) or Parkinsonian features.")
B.aLj=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Rotational Torticollis to the RIGHT with slight Laterocollis to the RIGHT.\n- **Palpation**: Hypertrophy and spasm of the Left Sternocleidomastoid (SCM).\n- **Motor**: Attempting to turn the head to the left meets significant resistance.\n- **Neurologic**: No weakness, ataxia, or craneal nerve deficits.")
B.aPJ=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture of involuntary sustained muscle contractions causing abnormal posture, relieved by a sensory geste, is diagnostic of **Cervical Dystonia (Spasmodic Torticollis)**.")
B.aN_=new A.k("+ Identify the specific muscles involved in a Right Rotational Torticollis.","To turn the head to the RIGHT, the active muscles are:\n1.  **Left Sternocleidomastoid (SCM)**: Contralateral rotator.\n2.  **Right Splenius Capitis**: Ipsilateral rotator.\n*Key Procedural Note*: These would be the primary targets for Chemodenervation.")
B.aK7=new A.k("+ Describe the Mechanism of Action of the first-line treatment (Botulinum Toxin Type A).","Botulinum Toxin A is a protease that cleaves **SNAP-25**, a protein in the **SNARE Complex**. This prevents the fusion of Acetylcholine vesicles with the presynaptic membrane at the neuromuscular junction, causing a reversible chemical denervation. \n*(Note: Type B cleaves VAMP/Synaptobrevin).* ")
B.aLb=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You plan for Chemodenervation. The patient asks about safety.")
B.aQl=new A.k("+ What is the 'Black Box Warning' for this medication, and how do you mitigate it?","The Boxed Warning is for **Distant Spread of Toxin**, which can cause dysphagia (swallowing difficulty) and respiratory compromise. \n- **Mitigation**: Use the lowest effective dose, avoid bilateral SCM injections (increases dysphagia risk), and utilize **Dual Guidance** (EMG to confirm motor endplate activity + Ultrasound to visualize vascular structures).")
B.aNy=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient returns 6 weeks later reporting *zero* improvement. This is her 4th injection cycle, and previous cycles worked well.")
B.aNW=new A.k("+ How do you manage this 'Secondary Non-Responder'?","I would suspect the development of **Neutralizing Antibodies** (Immunoresistance). \n- **Frontalis Test**: Inject a small test dose into the forehead. If it fails to paralyze the frontalis muscle (no wrinkle loss), antibodies are confirmed.\n- **Management**: Switch serotypes (e.g., from Type A to Type B - RimabotulinumtoxinB) or attempt a 'Drug Holiday' to allow antibody titers to fall.")
B.aOf=new A.k(u.j,"During the examination, the patient makes an inappropriate comment: 'I love the way your pants fit... can I take a picture of you?' She pulls out her phone.")
B.aO4=new A.k("+ Manage this breach of professional boundaries.","I would strictly enforce boundaries immediately.\nResponse: 'Ms. X, that comment and behavior are completely inappropriate. It breaches the professional boundary of our doctor-patient relationship. Please put your phone away. Because this boundary has been violated, I can no longer serve as your physician effective immediately. I will complete today's urgent medical assessment, but I will be transferring your care to another provider. I will provide you with emergency coverage for 30 days to ensure you are safe, but our therapeutic relationship is terminated.'")
B.aBy=s([B.aPn,B.aN5,B.aLj,B.aPJ,B.aN_,B.aK7,B.aLb,B.aQl,B.aNy,B.aNW,B.aOf,B.aO4],t.E)
B.PQ=new A.bk("neckpain","Cervical Dystonia (Torticollis)",B.aBy,!1)
B.aMA=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 71-year-old female with a recent Left MCA stroke (resulting in Right Hemiplegia) is seen on the acute inpatient rehabilitation unit. She complains of 2 weeks of worsening right shoulder pain that interferes with her sleep and therapy.")
B.aMk=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In assessing Hemiplegic Shoulder Pain (HSP), use the **'4 S's'** Framework:\n- **Subluxation**: Is there a palpable gap in the glenohumeral joint due to hypotonia?\n- **Spasticity**: Is high tone in the adductors/internal rotators (Pec Major, Lat Dorsi) causing impingement?\n- **Structural**: Pre-existing rotator cuff tear or adhesive capsulitis?\n- **Syndromic**: Signs of Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS Type 1 - Shoulder-Hand Syndrome).\n- **Prevention**: Ask about handling techniques (e.g., pulling on the arm during transfers) which often precipitates HSP.")
B.aPi=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: 1-fingerbreadth inferior subluxation of the right humeral head.\n- **Tone**: Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS) 3/4 in Right Pectoralis Major and Subscapularis.\n- **ROM**: Passive External Rotation is limited to 0 degrees (neutral) with a hard end-feel.\n- **Provocative Tests**: Neer's and Hawkins' signs are positive for impingement pain.\n- **Hand**: No edema or color changes (Ruling out CRPS).")
B.aO0=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of severe spasticity (Internal Rotators) and limited external rotation with a 'hard end-feel' suggests **Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder)** superimposed on **Spastic Hemiplegia**.")
B.aML=new A.k("+ Differentiate Spasticity from Contracture in this scenario.","- **Spasticity**: Velocity-dependent increase in tone (The faster you move, the tighter it gets). A 'Catch' is felt.\n- **Contracture**: Fixed resistance throughout the range, regardless of velocity. \n*Clinical Pearl*: This patient likely has both. The spastic internal rotators kept the arm immobile, leading to the capsular contracture.")
B.aPS=new A.k("+ Outline your comprehensive management plan.","1.  **Prevention/Positioning**: Ensure the arm is supported (lap tray or trough) to reduce subluxation strain. Educate staff: **NEVER use overhead pulleys** (high risk of impingement in hemiplegia).\n2.  **Spasticity Management**: Chemodenervation (Botulinum toxin/Phenol) to the Pectoralis Major/Latissimus Dorsi to 'unlocked' the shoulder.\n3.  **Analgesia**: Suprascapular Nerve Block (diagnostic and therapeutic) or Intra-articular steroid injection.\n4.  **Therapy**: Gentle, pain-free range of motion (AAROM). Avoid aggressive stretching.")
B.aQH=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You decide to proceed with a Glenohumeral Intra-articular Corticosteroid injection to treat the adhesive capsulitis component.")
B.aKc=new A.k("+ Describe your preferred injection technique.","I would perform an **Ultrasound-Guided Posterior Approach**.\n1.  **Patient Position**: Seated, arm resting in lap.\n2.  **Probe Placement**: Transverse axis over the posterior glenohumeral joint (infraspinatus fossa).\n3.  **Target**: The needle enters lateral-to-medial (in-plane), targeting the gap between the humeral head and posterior labrum.\n4.  **Confirmation**: Visualize the needle tip within the joint space and observe intra-articular flow of the injectate (hydro-dissection) to ensure 100% accuracy and avoid injecting the rotator cuff tendon.")
B.aLe=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's therapist approaches you with a printed article. 'Doctor, I read about this 'High Volume Hydrodilatation' procedure where you inject 40mL of fluid to break the scary tissue. It says it works better than regular shots. Can you do that for her today? She needs it now!'")
B.aM1=new A.k(u.j,"Role-play your response to the therapist's request for a procedure you are not credentialed/trained to perform.")
B.aL2=new A.k("+ Your response:","I would validate their advocacy but firmly hold the boundary of **Scope of Competence**.\nResponse: 'I appreciate you researching advanced options for our patient; it shows how much you care about her recovery. While Hydrodilatation is a valid procedure described in literature, it carries higher risks (capsular rupture, pain flare) and requires specific training and often fluoroscopic guidance which I do not currently possess. **Ethically, I cannot perform a procedure I am not fully competent in**, as that puts the patient at risk of harm (Non-maleficence). I will proceed with the standard evidence-based steroid injection today, which I am fully expert in. If that fails, we can discuss a referral to an interventionalist who specializes in high-volume procedures.'")
B.aDm=s([B.aMA,B.aMk,B.aPi,B.aO0,B.aML,B.aPS,B.aQH,B.aKc,B.aLe,B.aM1,B.aL2],t.E)
B.Q0=new A.bk("poststroke-shoulder-pain","Hemiplegic Shoulder Pain (HSP)",B.aDm,!1)
B.aOW=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 46-year-old female presents with subacute, progressive proximal weakness over 3 months. She reports difficulty rising from a chair, combing her hair, and carrying groceries. She denies fevers, but notes a 'sunburn-like' rash on her eyelids and knuckles.")
B.aPY=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In assessing proximal weakness, differentiate **Myopathy** (Muscle) from **Neuropathy** (Nerve):\n- **Distribution**: Proximal > Distal suggests Myopathy. (Distal > Proximal suggests Neuropathy).\n- **Sensation**: Myopathies are strictly motor (no numbness/tingling).\n- **Pain**: Polymyositis/DM are often *painless* or have mild soreness. Severe pain suggests Polymyalgia Rheumatica (PMR) or Viral Myositis.\n- **Cutaneous**: Heliotrope rash (violaceous eyelids), Gottron's Papules (knuckles), or Shawl Sign (V-neck rash).\n- **Systemic**: Dysphagia (esophageal muscle), Dyspnea (Interstitial Lung Disease), or Raynaud's phenomenon.")
B.aMx=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Skin**: Faint violet discoloration of the upper eyelids and scaly erythematous papules over the MCP joints.\n- **Motor**: \n    - Deltoids/Biceps: 4/5.\n    - Hip Flexors/Quads: 3+/5.\n    - Distal strength (Grip, Ankle): 5/5.\n- **Functional**: Positive Gower's Sign (uses hands to push off thighs when standing).\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ and symmetric (Preserved in myopathy, lost in neuropathy).")
B.aOX=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of proximal weakness, preserved reflexes, and specific cutaneous findings (Heliotrope, Gottron's) is diagnostic of **Dermatomyositis (DM)**.")
B.aOG=new A.k("+ Diagnostic Findings Return: CPK is 4,500. EMG shows 'Early Recruitment of short-duration, small-amplitude MUAPs' with fibrillations. Muscle Biopsy shows 'Perifascicular Atrophy'.","**Pathology Correlate**: \n- **Dermatomyositis**: Perifascicular Atrophy + Perimysial inflammation (CD4+ T-cells). \n- *(Contrast with Polymyositis: Endomysial inflammation/CD8+ T-cells)*.")
B.aMS=new A.k("+ With a diagnosis of Dermatomyositis confirmed, what is your most critical Next Step in management?","**Malignancy Screening**. \nAdult-onset Dermatomyositis has a strong paraneoplastic association (Ovarian, Lung, GI, Breast). \n- **Workup**: CT Chest/Abdomen/Pelvis, age-appropriate cancer screening (Mammogram, PAP, Colonoscopy), and consideration of specific antibodies (Anti-TIF1-gamma associated with cancer).")
B.aMT=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You treat her with high-dose Prednisone. Her CPK normalizes (drops to 200), but 2 months later, she returns complaining that her legs feel *weaker* and she can no longer climb stairs.")
B.aO1=new A.k("+ Differentiate between a 'Disease Flare' and 'Steroid Myopathy'.","- **Steroid Myopathy**: Normal CPK + Worsening Proximal Weakness (Type 2 fiber atrophy). \n- **Flare**: Elevated CPK + Worsening Weakness.\n*Action*: Since her CPK is normal, this is Steroid Myopathy. We must **taper steroids** rapidly and switch to a steroid-sparing agent (Methotrexate/Azathioprine).")
B.aKf=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You recommend starting Methotrexate to spare steroids. She refuses: 'That's a chemotherapy drug! I don't have cancer! I'm not taking poison.'")
B.aKA=new A.k(u.j,"Role-play your counseling to address her fear of the medication label.")
B.aLq=new A.k("+ Your response:","I would de-escalate her fear by framing the dosage.\nResponse: 'I completely understand your reaction\u2014seeing that label is scary. While Methotrexate is used in high doses for cancer to kill cells, we use it in **tiny, once-a-week doses** as an 'Immunomodulator' to gently calm down the immune system that is attacking your muscles. In this low dose, it acts like a dimmer switch for inflammation, not a poison. It allows us to get you off the steroids, which cause the bone loss and skin thinning you want to avoid. We will monitor your labs closely to ensure it stays safe.'")
B.aBF=s([B.aOW,B.aPY,B.aMx,B.aOX,B.aOG,B.aMS,B.aMT,B.aO1,B.aKf,B.aKA,B.aLq],t.E)
B.PU=new A.bk("progressiveweakness","Dermatomyositis & Inflammatory Myopathy",B.aBF,!1)
B.aNs=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 16-year-old elite high school baseball pitcher presents with 3 weeks of worsening medial elbow pain. He reports a decrease in velocity and control, but denies a specific 'pop' event.")
B.aLM=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In the throwing athlete, your history must quantify the **Workload**:\n- **Volume**: Pitches per game? Games per week? Showcase events?\n- **Mechanics**: Does he throw curveballs/sliders? (Higher torque).\n- **Neurologic**: Any numbness in the 4th/5th digits? (Ulnar Neuritis often co-occurs).\n- **'Dead Arm'**: Has he lost velocity or accuracy recently? (Early sign of UCL failure).\n- **Pop**: A distinct pop suggests an acute rupture vs. chronic attenuation.")
B.aKW=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Mild effusion over the medial epicondyle. No bruising.\n- **Palpation**: Tender at the sublime tubercle (UCL insertion).\n- **Stability**: \n    - **Valgus Stress Test**: Pain and laxity at 30 degrees flexion (isolates UCL) vs. 0 degrees (bony stability).\n    - **Milking Maneuver**: Positive for apprehension and pain.\n- **Neurologic**: Positive Tinel's at the Cubital Tunnel. 5/5 strength in FDP 4/5 and intrinsics.")
B.aLR=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of medial instability (Valgus stress/Milking) and performance decline in a pitcher is diagnostic of **Ulnar Collateral Ligament (UCL) Insufficiency**.")
B.aMR=new A.k("+ Compare the diagnostic utility of MRI vs. Dynamic Ultrasound for this condition.","- **MRI Arthrogram**: The gold standard for visualizing the tissue quality and partial tears ('T-Sign').\n- **Dynamic Ultrasound**: Superior for assessing **Functional Laxity**. We can stress the joint in real-time and measure the gapping (widening) of the joint line compared to the contralateral side. >1mm asymmetry is significant.")
B.aKw=new A.k("+ Imaging confirms a high-grade partial tear of the UCL. Outline your Rehabilitation Plan.","1.  **Acute Phase**: Complete rest from throwing (Active Rest). Control inflammation/pain.\n2.  **Recovery Phase**: Focus on Kinetic Chain mechanics (Core/Hips) and Scapular dyskinesis.\n3.  **Strengthening**: Initiate the **'Thrower's Ten'** program (rotator cuff/scapular stabilizers).\n4.  **Return to Play**: Begin **Interval Throwing Program (ITP)** only when pain-free and strength is symmetric. Progress from 45ft -> 60ft -> 90ft -> 120ft -> Mound.")
B.aN3=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient returns 3 months later. He is throwing pain-free but is frustrated that his velocity hasn't fully returned. He asks about 'Tommy John Surgery' to make him throw faster.")
B.aKz=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You examine the league notes and see he was throwing 120 pitches/game on back-to-back days.")
B.aL7=new A.k("+ Counsel the coach/parent on Pitch Smart Guidelines (MLB/USA Baseball).","I would educate them: 'The UCL is a ligament, not a muscle\u2014it cannot simply be 'trained' to take infinite load. Evidence shows that **Pitch Counts** are the specific safety limit. For a 16-year-old, the limit is ~95 pitches/game, with **4 days rest** mandated afterwards. Exceeding this or pitching through fatigue is the #1 predictor of surgery. 'Tommy John' reconstructs the ligament but does not guarantee a return to elite velocity.'")
B.aPO=new A.k(u.j,"The patient confides in you: 'Doc, I know I'm not supposed to, but I started taking Anavar (Oxandrolone) to get my strength back. My gym buddy gets it for me. Please don't tell my parents.'")
B.aQt=new A.k("+ Manage this confession of Anabolic Steroid use in a minor.","I would balance Confidentiality with Safety.\nResponse: 'I appreciate you trusting me enough to tell me. This is a crucial piece of your medical history. \n1.  **Medical Reality**: Steroids might build muscle, but they weaken tendons/ligaments (making a UCL tear *more* likely) and risk permanent heart/liver damage.\n2.  **Legal/Safety**: As a minor, I generally respect your privacy, but if you are using a controlled substance that puts you in immediate danger, I have a duty to ensure you are safe. I strongly encourage we bring your parents into this conversation so we can monitor your health (liver enzymes/heart) together. I won't call the police, but I cannot ignore the medical risk this poses to your life.'")
B.aDq=s([B.aNs,B.aLM,B.aKW,B.aLR,B.aMR,B.aKw,B.aN3,B.aKz,B.aL7,B.aPO,B.aQt],t.E)
B.PL=new A.bk("rightelbowpain","Ulnar Collateral Ligament (UCL) Injury",B.aDq,!1)
B.aLS=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 61-year-old male accountant presents with 4 months of progressive right foot drop. He initially noticed catching his toe on carpet, but now reports an audible 'slap' when walking. He also mentions some clumsiness with his keys.")
B.aKO=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating painless weakness, assess for **Upper vs. Lower Motor Neuron** involvement:\n- **UMN**: Stiffness, spasticity, jaw jerk, pseudo-bulbar affect (uncontrollable laughing/crying).\n- **LMN**: Fasciculations (twitches), cramps, atrophy.\n- **Regions**: Screen for Bulbar (swallowing/speech), Cervical (hands), Thoracic (breathing), and Lumbosacral (walking) involvement.\n- **Red Flags**: Weight loss, morning headache (hypercapnia from respiratory failure).")
B.aMC=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Gait**: Steppage gait on the right with foot slap.\n- **Inspection**: Atrophy of the Right First Dorsal Interosseous (FDI) and Abductor Pollicis Brevis (APB), with sparing of the Abductor Digiti Minimi (ADM) = **'Split Hand Sign'**.\n- **Motor**: \n    - Right Dorsiflexion: 3/5.\n    - Right EHL: 3/5.\n    - Bilateral Grip: 4/5.\n- **Reflexes**: 3+ (Hyperreflexic) throughout, including the weak right leg. Positive Babinski bilaterally. Jaw Jerk positive.\n- **Sensation**: Intact to all modalities (Ruling out polyneuropathy).")
B.aM7=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of **sensation-sparing** weakness with simultaneous **UMN signs** (Hyperreflexia/Babinski) and **LMN signs** (Atrophy/Fasciculations) in multiple regions is pathognomonic for **Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)**.")
B.aP9=new A.k("+ Diagnostic Findings: EMG shows acute and chronic denervation in 3 regions (Cervical, Thoracic, Lumbosacral). How does this classify under the El Escorial Criteria?","Evidence of UMN + LMN signs in **3 distinct regions** classifies this as **Definite ALS** according to the Revised El Escorial Criteria. \n*(Note: 2 regions = Probable ALS; 1 region = Possible ALS).*")
B.aKR=new A.k("+ What is your immediate respiratory safety screen?","I would measure **Forced Vital Capacity (FVC)** both **Upright and Supine**. \n- A drop of >10% in Supine FVC indicates diaphragm weakness.\n- FVC <50% predicts respiratory failure and indicates the need for non-invasive ventilation (BiPAP).")
B.aP5=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You initiate disease-modifying therapy.")
B.aKy=new A.k("+ Describe the Mechanism of Action for the two primary FDA-approved oral agents.","1.  **Riluzole**: Glutamate inhibitor (prevents excitotoxicity). Extends survival by ~2-3 months. Monitor LFTs.\n2.  **Edaravone**: Free radical scavenger (antioxidant). Slows functional decline.")
B.aO3=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is devastated. 'Am I going to die? How long do I have?'")
B.aOD=new A.k(u.j,"Role-play Breaking Bad News using the **SPIKES Protocol**.")
B.aNV=new A.k("+ Your response:","**S (Setting/Setup)**: Ensure privacy, sit down, involve family.\n**P (Perception)**: 'What have you been told so far about your condition?'\n**I (Invitation)**: 'Is it okay if I share the results with you now?'\n**K (Knowledge)**: 'I am afraid the news is serious. The tests confirm you have ALS. This is a disease where the motor neurons disconnect from the muscles.'\n**E (Emotions)**: *Silence*. Allow the patient to cry/react. Validate: 'I can see this is a huge shock.'\n**S (Strategy/Summary)**: 'We cannot cure this, but we will not abandon you. We have a multidisciplinary team (Speech, PT, OT, Nutrition) to keep you independent as long as possible. We will fight for your quality of life together.'")
B.aL5=new A.k("+ Critical Safety Check:","Given the terminal diagnosis, I must screen for **Suicidal Ideation**. 'This is heavy news. Sometimes people feel like life isn't worth living when they hear this. Have you had any thoughts of hurting yourself?'")
B.aDt=s([B.aLS,B.aKO,B.aMC,B.aM7,B.aP9,B.aKR,B.aP5,B.aKy,B.aO3,B.aOD,B.aNV,B.aL5],t.E)
B.PS=new A.bk("rightfootdrop","Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)",B.aDt,!1)
B.aLu=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 58-year-old female presents with 4 months of nocturnal right hand numbness. She reports waking up shaking her hand out ('Flick Sign') and dropping her coffee cup in the mornings.")
B.aQv=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating hand paresthesias, define the **Anatomic Distribution**:\n- **Median**: Thumb, Index, Middle, and lateral Ring finger. (Palmar cutaneous branch spares the palm).\n- **Ulnar**: Little finger and medial Ring finger.\n- **Radial**: Dorsum of the hand/thumb.\n- **C6 Radiculopathy**: Thumb/Index finger + Neck pain + Biceps weakness.\n- **Proximal Mimics**: Pronator Syndrome (forearm pain + palmar numbness).")
B.aKZ=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Mild atrophy of the Right Thenar Eminence (Abductor Pollicis Brevis).\n- **Sensation**: Decreased light touch in digits 1-3. **Palm sensation is intact** (Palmar cutaneous branch passes *over* the tunnel).\n- **Provocative Tests**: \n    - **Phalen's**: Positive at 30 seconds.\n    - **Durkan's Compression**: Positive at 15 seconds (Most sensitive).\n    - **Tinel's**: Positive at the wrist.\n- **Motor**: Weakness (4/5) in Resisted Thumb Abduction (APB).")
B.aNN=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The classic splitting of the ring finger (lateral half numb), nocturnal symptoms, and preserved palmar sensation is diagnostic of **Carpal Tunnel Syndrome (CTS)**.")
B.aNz=new A.k("+ You order an Ultrasound of the wrist. What findings support the diagnosis?","In CTS, the Median Nerve becomes swollen proximal to the compression.\n- **Cross-Sectional Area (CSA)**: Measurement > **12 mm\xb2** (at the proximal carpal tunnel inlet) is highly sensitive and specific.")
B.aNc=new A.k("+ You proceed to Electrodiagnostics (EMG/NCS) to grade the severity. Define the grades.","1.  **Mild**: Sensory slowing only (Prolonged DSL / Decreased SNAP amplitude).\n2.  **Moderate**: Motor slowing (Prolonged Distal Motor Latency > 4.2ms).\n3.  **Severe**: Axonal loss (Fibrillations on EMG / Absent SNAPs / Low CMAP amplitude).")
B.aMQ=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","NCS confirms **Severe CTS** with active denervation (fibrillations) in the APB muscle.")
B.aM9=new A.k("+ How does the finding of 'Severe' disease change your management compared to Mild/Moderate?","For **Severe CTS** with axonal loss/thenar atrophy, conservative care (splinting/injections) is insufficient. \n- **Recommendation**: Early surgical referral for **Carpal Tunnel Release** is indicated to prevent permanent motor loss. Injections may provide temporary relief but delaying decompression risks irreversible atrophy.")
B.aK5=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks: 'Doc, I've been a secretary typing on a keyboard for 30 years. My job definitely caused this. Can you sign these Workers Comp papers saying my typing caused my CTS?'")
B.aPN=new A.k(u.j,"Address the patient's request regarding Causation.")
B.aQD=new A.k("+ Your response:","I would answer based on Epidemiological Evidence, not assumption.\nResponse: 'I understand you feel your work is the cause, but I have to follow the medical evidence to be accurate on these legal forms. Large scale studies (like NIOSH) have consistently shown that **computer typing is NOT a strong risk factor** for Carpal Tunnel Syndrome. The strong risk factors are **forceful gripping, vibration, and high-repetition wrist deviation** (like meatpacking or assembly line work), as well as personal factors like diabetes or thyroid issues. I can state that you have CTS, but I cannot medically certify that typing *caused* it, as the science doesn't support that link.'")
B.anW=s([B.aLu,B.aQv,B.aKZ,B.aNN,B.aNz,B.aNc,B.aMQ,B.aM9,B.aK5,B.aPN,B.aQD],t.E)
B.PN=new A.bk("righthandnumbness","Carpal Tunnel Syndrome (CTS)",B.anW,!1)
B.aLY=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 60-year-old male presents with 3 months of worsening right knee pain, particularly when walking down stairs. He reports intermittent swelling and a 'grinding' sensation.")
B.aOd=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating the older adult knee, distinguish **Mechanical** vs. **Inflammatory** pain:\n- **Mechanical**: Worsens with activity, 'locking' or 'catching' (Meniscal tear), 'giving way' (Instability/Weakness).\n- **Inflammatory**: Morning stiffness > 30 mins, warmth, redness (Rheumatoid/Gout).\n- **Location**: Medial (common OA), Anterior (Patellofemoral), Lateral.\n- **Risk Factors**: Previous trauma, obesity, occupation (kneeling/squatting).")
B.aNX=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Mild varus deformity (Bow-legged) and small effusion.\n- **Palpation**: Medial Joint Line tenderness. Crepitus with localized flexion/extension.\n- **ROM**: 0-120 degrees, painful at end-range flexion.\n- **Special Tests**:\n    - **Thessaly's**: Positive for medial pain (Meniscal provocation).\n    - **McMurray's**: Positive click/pain medial joint line.\n    - **Ligaments**: Lachman/Varus/Valgus are stable.")
B.aL3=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of age, mechanical symptoms, measuring joint line tenderness, and crepitus suggests **Medial Compartment Osteoarthritis** with a likely **Degenerative Meniscus Tear**.")
B.aPh=new A.k("+ You order X-rays. Describe the Kellgren-Lawrence (K-L) Grading System.","- **Grade 1**: Doubtful narrowing, possible osteophytes.\n- **Grade 2**: **Definite osteophytes**, possible narrowing. (This is the diagnostic threshold for OA).\n- **Grade 3**: Moderate narrowing, multiple osteophytes, sclerosis.\n- **Grade 4**: Large osteophytes, **severe narrowing (bone-on-bone)**, deformity.")
B.aOZ=new A.k("+ MRI confirms a 'Horizontal Cleavage Tear' of the Medial Meniscus. The patient asks for a 'clean out' surgery (Arthroscopy). How do you counsel him?","I would advise **AGAINST** arthroscopy.\n- **Evidence**: The **METEOR Trial** (and others) showed that for *Degenerative* Meniscal Tears in the setting of OA, Arthroscopy with Partial Meniscectomy provides **NO long-term benefit** over Physical Therapy alone and may accelerate OA progression. \n- **Indication for Surgery**: True 'Mechanical Block' (Locked knee) from a displaced bucket-handle tear.")
B.aLH=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You proceed with conservative management. He asks about injections.")
B.aPx=new A.k("+ Compare Corticosteroid vs. Hyaluronic Acid (Viscosupplementation) injections.","- **Corticosteroids**: Rapid onset (days), short duration (weeks). Best for acute inflammatory flares/effusions. Risk of cartilage toxicity with frequent use.\n- **Hyaluronic Acid**: Slower onset (weeks), longer duration (months). Acts as a lubricant/shock absorber. Best for mild-moderate OA (K-L 2-3) without acute inflammation.")
B.aLN=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is scheduled for a Partial Colectomy next month. He asks: 'Can I just get my Knee Replacement done the same week since I'll be off work anyway?'")
B.aOY=new A.k("+ Recommmendation:","**ABSOLUTELY NOT**. \n- **Perioperative Risk**: Elective Arthroplasty (TKA) is contraindicated in the setting of recent or concurrent abdominal surgery due to the high risk of **hematogenous seeding** (translocation of gut bacteria) causing a Periprosthetic Joint Infection (PJI). \n- **Timeline**: He must wait until fully recovered from the colectomy (typically 3-6 months) before elective orthopedic surgery.")
B.aKG=new A.k(u.j,"The patient is frustrated with his pain. 'PT is a waste of time. My wife has Oxycodone for her back, and I took one and it helped. Just write me a script for like 30 of those so I can function.'")
B.aKB=new A.k("+ Manage this request for Opioids.","I would firmly adhere to Opioid Stewardship guidelines.\nResponse: 'I'm glad you found relief, but I cannot prescribe Oxycodone for this. \n1.  **Safety**: Taking medication prescribed to others is unsafe and illegal.\n2.  **Standards**: Guidelines are clear that **Opioids are NOT indicated** for Osteoarthritis as they actually worsen pain over time (Hyperalgesia) and carry addiction risk. \n3.  **Plan**: We will use safer, effective options: Topical NSAIDs (Voltaren), Cymbalta (blocks nerve pain), or injections. I want to treat your pain, but I must do it safely.'")
B.aDz=s([B.aLY,B.aOd,B.aNX,B.aL3,B.aPh,B.aOZ,B.aLH,B.aPx,B.aLN,B.aOY,B.aKG,B.aKB],t.E)
B.Pt=new A.bk("rightkneepain","Knee Osteoarthritis & Meniscal Pathology",B.aDz,!1)
B.aPX=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 24-year-old female with T4 ASIA B Paraplegia (1 month post-injury) complains of sudden onset shortness of breath and a 'pounding' headache. She appears flushed and anxious.")
B.aLr=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a high-level SCI patient (T6 or above), acute symptoms require ruling out **Autonomic Dysreflexia** vs. **Pulmonary Embolism**:\n- **AD Signs**: Pounding headache, diaphoresis/flushing *above* the level of injury, nasal congestion, anxiety ('Sense of Impending Doom').\n- **PE Signs**: Pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, hypoxia.\n- **Triggers**: \n    - **Bladder** (Most common): Kinked catheter, retention, UTI.\n    - **Bowel**: Constipation, impaction.\n    - **Skin**: Pressure ulcer, ingrown toenail, tight clothing.")
B.aNQ=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 150/90 (Baseline 90/60). HR 52 (Bradycardia). SpO2 96% RA.\n- **Neuro**: Flushing of the face/neck. Pale/cool skin below T4.\n- **Abdomen**: Palpable bladder distension.\n- **Lungs**: Clear to auscultation (Lower likelihood of PE/Pneumonia).")
B.aM2=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of **Relative Hypertension** (SBP > 20-40mmHg above baseline), **Bradycardia**, and **Headache** in a T4 SCI patient is diagnostic of **Autonomic Dysreflexia (AD)**.")
B.aQj=new A.k("+ Explain the Pathophysiology of the Bradycardia in this syndrome.","A noxious stimulus below the lesion triggers a massive **Sympathetic Surge** (Vasoconstriction -> Hypertension). \n- The Baroreceptors in the carotid/aortic arch detect the high BP and signal the Vagus Nerve to increase parasympathetic output to the heart.\n- Result: **Parasympathetic Bradycardia** attempts to compensate for the Sympathetic Hypertension.")
B.aLp=new A.k("+ Outline your Stepwise Management Algorithm.","1.  **Immediate Actions**: Sit the patient **UP** (induce orthostatic hypotension). Loosen tight clothing/abdominal binder.\n2.  **Source Control**: Check urinary drainage (unkink foley / straight cath). If negative, check for fecal impaction (with lidocaine jelly to avoid worsening stimulus).\n3.  **Pharmacology**: If SBP remains >150 after mechanical measures:\n    - **Nitropaste 1 inch** (topical - can be wiped off if BP crashes).\n    - **Nifedipine 10mg** (Immediate Release - 'Bite and Swallow') or **Captopril 25mg**.")
B.aKM=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You order the nurse to check for fecal impaction. The nurse refuses: 'That is not my job. I'm not doing a rectal exam. You can wait for the aide.'")
B.aNM=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's BP is now 170/100 and she is complaining of blurred vision (Risk of Retinal Hemorrhage/Stroke).")
B.aOQ=new A.k(u.j,"Manage this refusal of care in a hypertensive emergency.")
B.aKo=new A.k("+ Your response:","I would prioritize **Patient Safety** over hierarchy.\nResponse: 'I understand this is an unpleasant task, but this patient is in a Hypertensive Emergency. Delaying care puts her at risk for Stroke or Seizure *right now*. \n- **Step 1 (Direct Order)**: I am giving a direct medical order to perform this safety check immediately.\n- **Step 2 (Escalation)**: If you refuse, I will call the Charge Nurse/House Supervisor to document this refusal of emergency care.\n- **Step 3 (Physician Action)**: **I will perform the exam myself immediately**. The patient's life comes first. We will discuss the chain of command and professional responsibilities after she is safe.'")
B.arH=s([B.aPX,B.aLr,B.aNQ,B.aM2,B.aQj,B.aLp,B.aKM,B.aNM,B.aOQ,B.aKo],t.E)
B.PY=new A.bk("shortnessofbreath","Autonomic Dysreflexia (AD)",B.arH,!1)
B.aMq=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 40-year-old female with a T4 ASIA B Spinal Cord Injury (1 week post-injury) develops sudden sustained tachycardia (HR 130 bpm). She has a concurrent Subdural Hematoma (SDH) from her initial trauma.")
B.aQG=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a recumbent SCI patient, **Unexplained Tachycardia** is a Red Flag.\n- **Pain**: Is there a noxious stimulus below the lesion? (Though T4 usually causes Bradycardia in AD, tachycardia can occur).\n- **Respiration**: Oxygen desaturation? Pleuritic Chest Pain? (Often absent in SCI due to sensory loss).\n- **Volume**: Orthostatic hypotension vs. Hypovolemic shock (Bleeding?).\n- **Infection**: Fever? UTI symptoms?\n- **DVT Signs**: Unilateral leg swelling? (But remember: Calf pain/Homan's sign is absent in paralyzed limbs).")
B.aLZ=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: HR 130. BP 100/60. SpO2 93% RA. Temp 37.5C.\n- **Lungs**: Clear to auscultation.\n- **Extremities**: 2cm circumference difference in the Right Calf vs. Left. No erythema.\n- **Labs**: WBC 12.0 (Mildly elevated). D-Dimer: 4,500 (Elevated).")
B.aQ5=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of Tachycardia, Hypoxia, and Elevated D-Dimer in an immobile trauma patient is highly suspicious for **Pulmonary Embolism (PE)**.")
B.aKr=new A.k("+ Calculate the Modified Wells Score for PE risk stratification.","- **Initial Suspicion**: \n    - HR > 100 (+1.5)\n    - Immobilization/Surgery (+1.5)\n    - Clinical Signs of DVT (+3.0)\n    - PE most likely diagnosis (+3.0)\n- **Score**: >6.0 = **High Probability**.\n- **Action**: Immediate CT Angiogram (CTA) of the Chest.")
B.aNJ=new A.k("+ CT confirms a large Right Lower Lobe PE. How do you manage this in a patient with a recent Subdural Hematoma?","**Anticoagulation vs. Filter**: \n- **Contraindication**: Full systemic anticoagulation (Heparin/Lovenox) is **Contraindicated** due to the active/recent Traumatic Brain Injury (Risk of SDH expansion).\n- **Intervention**: Place a **Retrievable IVC Filter** immediately to prevent further emboli from the DVT shower.")
B.aNx=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","3 weeks later, the Neurosurgery team clears her for anticoagulation. The IVC filter is retrieved.")
B.aPZ=new A.k("+ Why is it critical to retrieve the IVC filter once anticoagulation is started?","- **Long-term Risk**: IVC filters left in place permanently have a high rate of complications: **Filter Thrombosis** (blocking the cava), **Migration**, and **Vessel Perforation**. They are valid *bridges*, not permanent solutions for transient risks.")
B.aMa=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is stabilized and transfers to rehab. She asks: 'Be honest, Doc. Am I ever going to walk again?'")
B.aOa=new A.k(u.j,"Provide a professional, realistic prognosis based on her C7 ASIA A (Complete) status.")
B.aLD=new A.k("+ Your response:","I would use the **'Independence vs. Ambulation'** frame.\nResponse: 'That is the most important question, and you deserve an honest answer based on the data. For a complete injury at the C7 level, the spinal cord connection is severed. Statistically, the chance of regaining functional walking is **less than 5%**. \nHowever, I want to be clear about what C7 can do: You have triceps function. This means you can transfer yourself, push a manual wheelchair, drive a modified car, and live independently. **You will not be bedbound.** Our goal in rehab is to maximize that independence so you can live a full life, even if using a wheelchair for mobility.'")
B.aDd=s([B.aMq,B.aQG,B.aLZ,B.aQ5,B.aKr,B.aNJ,B.aNx,B.aPZ,B.aMa,B.aOa,B.aLD],t.E)
B.PW=new A.bk("tachycardia","Pulmonary Embolism (PE) in SCI",B.aDd,!1)
B.aMJ=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 23-year-old female with L2 Myelomeningocele (Shunted for Hydrocephalus at birth) presents with new-onset urinary incontinence. She notes she is 'leaking more' between catheterizations.")
B.aMr=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a Spina Bifida patient, **New Incontinence ('Wet')** is a Red Flag for **Shunt Failure**.\n- **The Triad**: 'Wet, Wobbly, Wacky' (Incontinence, Gait instability, Cognitive change).\n- **Symptoms**: Headache? Nausea/Vomiting? Vision changes (Papilledema)?\n- **Bladder History**: Frequency of intermittent catheterization (CIC)? Last Urodynamics?\n- **Safety**: **Latex Allergy** status? (High prevalence in SB - maintain Latex-Free environment).")
B.aL4=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: Stable. Afebrile (Ruling out Sepsis/UTI).\n- **Neuro**: New mild confusion (Wacky). Upper extremity hyperreflexia (suggesting increased intracranial pressure affecting the corticospinal tracts).\n- **Shunt**: Palpate the reservoir (Does it refill?). Track along the tubing for step-offs/breaks.\n- **Skin**: Check for pressure ulcers (sensory level L2).")
B.aPd=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of **New Incontinence**, **Confusion**, and **Hyperreflexia** in a shunted patient is **Ventriculoperitoneal (VP) Shunt Malfunction** until proven otherwise.")
B.aO_=new A.k("+ What is your immediate diagnostic workup?","1.  **CT Head (Non-Contrast)**: To assess for increasing ventricular size compared to prior (Hydrocephalus).\n2.  **Shunt Series (X-rays)**: AP/Lateral views of Skull, Chest, Abdomen to look for disconnection or kinking of the tubing.\n3.  **Urinalysis/Culture**: To rule out UTI as a trigger for transient incontinence.")
B.aLG=new A.k("+ The CT shows enlarged ventricles. Neurosurgery is consulted. While hospitalized, you review her Urodynamics. What are the 'Hostile' bladder features you are looking for?","**Detrusor Sphincter Dyssynergia (DSD)** and **High Storage Pressures (>40 cmH2O)**.\n- These features indicate a high risk for **Upper Tract Deterioration** (Hydronephrosis/Renal Failure) due to reflux. \n- Management: Anticholinergics (Oxybutynin) + CIC to keep pressures low.")
B.aMM=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient undergoes a successful Shunt Revision. Her incontinence resolves. However, on discharge exam, you notice multiple bruises in various stages of healing on her arms.")
B.aKq=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","She admits: 'My boyfriend gets angry sometimes and throws things. But he loves me.' This is **Intimate Partner Violence (IPV)**.")
B.aLO=new A.k(u.j,"Address this disclosure using the **SAFE Protocol**.")
B.aNR=new A.k("+ Your response:","**S (Safety)**: 'Do you feel safe going home today?'\n**A (Afraid/Abused)**: 'Has he ever hit you, kicked you, or forced you to do things you didn't want to?'\n**F (Friends/Family)**: 'Do you have a safe place to go (parents/shelter) if you leave?'\n**E (Emergency Plan)**: 'If you are in danger, do you have a code work or a bag packed?'\n\n**Reporting (Crucial Board Distinction)**: \n- If she is a competent adult, **I cannot report this to police against her will** (unless there is a gunshot/knife wound or a child/elder at risk). \n- My role is to **Empower** and **Resource** (Social Work, Hotlines), not to violate her autonomy. \n- *'I am concerned for your safety. No one deserves to be hit. I can help connect you with a safe shelter, but the decision is yours.'*")
B.a6l=s([B.aMJ,B.aMr,B.aL4,B.aPd,B.aO_,B.aLG,B.aMM,B.aKq,B.aLO,B.aNR],t.E)
B.Px=new A.bk("urinaryincontinence","Spina Bifida & Neurogenic Bladder",B.a6l,!1)
B.aKp=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 5-year-old boy presents with 'clumsiness' and difficulty keeping up with his peers at recess. His mother notes he uses his hands to 'climb up his legs' when standing up from the floor.")
B.aPw=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a male child with proximal weakness, focus on **Developmental Motor Milestones**:\n- **Delayed Walking**: Did he walk after 18 months? (Common in DMD).\n- **Gower's Sign**: The maneuver described (using arms to extend the hip due to gluteal weakness).\n- **Family History**: Any maternal uncles with wheelchair use? (X-linked Recessive).\n- **Cognition**: Is there speech delay or intellectual disability? (Dystrophin is expressed in the brain).")
B.aNd=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: **Lumbar Hyperlordosis** and **Calf Pseudohypertrophy** (Fatty infiltration, not true muscle).\n- **Gait**: Waddle gait (Trendelenburg) and Toe Walking (Heel cord contracture).\n- **Labs**: **CK (Creatine Kinase)** is markedly elevated (>10-50x normal) even before symptoms are severe.")
B.aMW=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of **Male Gender**, **Gower's Sign**, **Calf Pseudohypertrophy**, and **High CK** is diagnostic of **Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD)**.")
B.aOB=new A.k("+ Genetic testing confirms a Dystrophin mutation (Exon deletion). Discuss the Systems Review priorities.","DMD is a multi-system disease:\n1.  **Cardiac**: Dilated Cardiomyopathy is the leading cause of death. Requires **Cardiac MRI/Echo** at diagnosis and annually.\n2.  **Pulmonary**: Restrictive lung disease. Monitor FVC annually.\n3.  **Bone Health**: Risk of Osteoporosis (Steroid use + Immobility). Monitor Vitamin D/DEXA.\n4.  **Nutrition**: Risk of obesity (Steroids) vs. dysphagia (late stage).")
B.aQw=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You initiate Glucocorticoid therapy to prolong ambulation.")
B.aLw=new A.k("+ Compare the two primary steroid options: Prednisone vs. Deflazacort.","- **Prednisone**: \n    - Cheap/Available.\n    - **Side Effect**: Significant Weight Gain (Cushingoid) -> Worse functional load on weak muscles.\n- **Deflazacort**: \n    - **Benefit**: Less weight gain.\n    - **Risk**: Higher risk of **Cataracts** and **Growth Delay**. Often preferred if weight gain becomes a limiting factor.")
B.aOb=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Since this is an X-Linked Recessive condition, who else needs your attention?")
B.aOF=new A.k("+ Recommendation:","The **Mother**. \n- she requires **Genetic Counseling** to discuss carrier status (risk for future pregnancies).\n- **Carrier Health**: Female carriers can develop **Cardiomyopathy** even without skeletal muscle weakness. She needs a cardiology referral for herself.")
B.aN2=new A.k(u.j,"As you leave, the 5-year-old boy pulls on your coat and whispers: 'My friend's brother said I'm gonna die. Is that true?'")
B.aOI=new A.k("+ Manage this pediatric ethics scenario.","Do not lie, but do not traumatize. Use a **Parent-Bridging Strategy**.\nResponse: \n1.  **Validate**: 'That is a very scary thing to hear. I am sorry someone said that to you.'\n2.  **Age-Appropriate Truth**: 'Your muscles are different, and the doctors are working very hard to keep them strong. We have medicines now that we didn't have before.'\n3.  **Bridge**: 'This is a big question. I want to talk with your mom and dad so we can all answer this together in a way that makes sense.' \n*(Rationale: Parents have the right to determine when/how terminal prognosis is disclosed to a young child).*")
B.aBo=s([B.aKp,B.aPw,B.aNd,B.aMW,B.aOB,B.aQw,B.aLw,B.aOb,B.aOF,B.aN2,B.aOI],t.E)
B.PJ=new A.bk("weaknessinachild","Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD)",B.aBo,!1)
B.aKh=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 39-year-old male presents with acute right wrist drop. He admits to consuming 'a lot of alcohol' at a party last night and woke up with the weakness.")
B.aLm=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In evaluating **Wrist Drop**, localize the lesion based on spared muscles:\n- **Posterior Cord**: Deltoids/Latissimus Dorsi weak.\n- **Axilla**: Triceps weak.\n- **Spiral Groove** (Humerus): **Triceps SPARED** (branches exit proximally), but Brachioradialis/Wrist Extensors weak.\n- **Posterior Interosseous Nerve (PIN)**: **Supinator SPARED** (no sensory loss), only finger extension weak.\n- **Red Flag**: Trauma? Must rule out **Humeral Shaft Fracture**.")
B.aOc=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Motor**: \n    - Triceps: 5/5 (Intact -> Lesion is distal to Axilla).\n    - Brachioradialis: 0/5.\n    - Wrist Extension: 1/5.\n    - Finger Extension: 0/5.\n- **Sensation**: Decreased light touch over the **Dorsal First Webspace** (Radial sensory autonomic zone). Intact sensation in the axillary patch.\n- **Reflexes**: Triceps 2+, Brachioradialis 0.")
B.aQ0=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of **Spared Triceps**, **Weak Brachioradialis**, and **Dorsal Webspace Numbness** fits a **Radial Neuropathy at the Spiral Groove**.")
B.aNm=new A.k("+ You suspect compression ('Saturday Night Palsy'), but he also has a bruise on his arm. What imaging is mandatory?","**X-Ray of the Humerus**. \n- **Rationale**: Must rule out a **Holstein-Lewis Fracture** (Distal 1/3 Humeral Shaft Fracture) which can entrap or lacerate the radial nerve. Managing this as simple compression without X-ray would be malpractice.")
B.aMi=new A.k("+ X-ray is negative. You order NCS. How do you differentiate Conduction Block (Neurapraxia) vs. Axonal Loss?","Perform **Short Segment Stimulation ('Inching')** across the Spiral Groove.\n- **Neurapraxia**: Clean conduction block (Drop in CMAP amplitude proximal to the groove, normal distal).\n- **Axonotmesis**: Low CMAP amplitude everywhere (distal and proximal) due to wallerian degeneration.")
B.aKF=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Diagnosis is Neurapraxia (Good prognosis). You prescribe a splint.")
B.aKH=new A.k("+ Compare a Standard Cock-Up Splint vs. a Dynamic Extension Splint.","- **Wrist Cock-Up**: Keeps wrist neutral but fingers still droop (making function impossible). Good for night.\n- **Dynamic Extension Splint** (e.g., Thomas Splint): Uses rubber band outriggers to passively extend the fingers, allowing the patient to **actively grasp** and use the hand during the day. This is superior for functional independence.")
B.aM5=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Three months later, his strength returns to 4/5. He asks: 'Doc, I can't go back to my warehouse job yet. It feels weird. Can you sign me off on permanent disability?'")
B.aQI=new A.k(u.j,"Address the request for Disability vs. Return to Work.")
B.aPm=new A.k("+ Your response:","I would pivot to the **Therapeutic Benefit of Work**.\nResponse: 'I am glad your strength is returning (4/5). I cannot medically certify permanent disability because your condition is improving and you have functional hand use. \nHowever, I will write a strict **Graded Return to Work** plan:\n1.  **Restrictions**: No lifting >10lbs, frequent breaks.\n2.  **Goal**: Getting back to work\u2014even light duty\u2014actually speeds up recovery by keeping you active and preventing the depression that often comes with long-term disability. Let's focus on what you *can* do, rather than what you can't.'")
B.aDP=s([B.aKh,B.aLm,B.aOc,B.aQ0,B.aNm,B.aMi,B.aKF,B.aKH,B.aM5,B.aQI,B.aPm],t.E)
B.PZ=new A.bk("wristdrop","Radial Neuropathy ('Saturday Night Palsy')",B.aDP,!1)
B.aQm=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 65-year-old male presents to your outpatient clinic for cardiac rehabilitation evaluation. He is 2 weeks status-post Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG x3). He reports feeling 'weak' and notes a 'clicking' sensation in his chest when he rolls out of bed.")
B.aPP=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a post-sternotomy patient, differentiate **Musculoskeletal Pain** from **Ischemic Pain**:\n- **Pain Character**: Is it reproducible with movement/palpation (Sternal) or exertional/pressure (Angina)?\n- **Sternal Stability**: Any clicking, popping, or shifting of the bone?\n- **Infection**: Fever, drainage from the incision?\n- **Arrhythmia**: Palpitations, lightheadedness, or syncope?\n- **Psychosocial**: Screen for **Cardiac Depression** (very common post-CABG).")
B.aNU=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 110/70, HR 88 (Regular), SpO2 96%.\n- **Incision**: Clean, dry, well-approximated staples. No erythema.\n- **Sternum**: Palpation reveals mild tenderness but **No Instability** (No clicking/movement on coughing or Valsalva). The 'clicking' is likely soft tissue shifting, not non-union.\n- **Lungs**: Clear. No pleural effusion signs.\n- **Extremities**: Trace edema at the saphenous vein harvest site (Left leg).")
B.aNp=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient is stable for rehabilitation. The 'clicking' is benign, but he needs strict Biomechanical Precautions to prevent dehiscence.")
B.aPp=new A.k("+ Explain the modern 'Sternal Precautions' protocol.","We have moved away from strict 'No Lifting >5 lbs' which causes fear-avoidance. \nUse **'Keep Your Move in the Tube'** (Source: *Baylor Scott & White Protocol*):\n- Imagine a tube around your torso. You can lift heavier loads if you keep your elbows tucked close to your body ('Short Levers').\n- Avoid: Bilateral shoulder abduction + external rotation (stretching the peculiars) and unilateral heavy lifting.\n- **Goal**: Minimize shear forces on the sternum while allowing functional mobility.")
B.aOy=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You prescribe **Phase II Cardiac Rehabilitation**.")
B.aKb=new A.k("+ Define the Phases of Cardiac Rehab.","1.  **Phase I (Inpatient)**: Begins in ICU/Acute ward. Focus on early mobilization to prevent deconditioning. (1-2 METs).\n2.  **Phase II (Outpatient - Monitored)**: The current phase. Physician-supervised, EKG-monitored exercise (3x/week for 12 weeks). Focus on aerobic capacity and risk factor modification.\n3.  **Phase III/IV (Maintenance)**: Lifetime community-based exercise. Self-pay, non-monitored.")
B.aMy=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks: 'Doc, my wife and I... when can we be intimate again? And when can I drive?'")
B.aOO=new A.k("+ Provide a data-driven answer regarding Functional Capacity (METs).","1.  **Sexual Activity**: Requires **3-5 METs** (Source: *AHA Scientific Statement on Sexual Activity*). \n    - **Analogy**: 'If you can walk up **two flights of stairs** comfortably without chest pain or severe shortness of breath, your heart is strong enough for sexual activity.'\n2.  **Driving**: Typically restricted for **4 weeks** post-sternotomy. This is due to the risk of airbag impact on the healing bone, not just reaction time.")
B.aQd=new A.k(u.j,"The patient becomes tearful. 'I survived the surgery, but I feel empty. I don't want to do anything. Maybe I should have just died.'")
B.aNn=new A.k("+ Manage this disclosure of Suicidality/Depression.","1.  **Safety Check**: 'Do you have a plan to hurt yourself right now?' (If yes -> Emergency PSI). \n2.  **Normalization**: 'What you are feeling is called **Cardiac Depression**. It happens to up to 40% of patients after bypass surgery due to the inflammation and major life event. It is biologically real, not a weakness.'\n3.  **Plan**: 'We will treat this. Cardiac Rehab itself helps (exercise is an antidepressant), but I also want to refer you to our cardiac psychologist.' \n*(Rationale: Depression is an independent risk factor for mortality post-MI/CABG).*")
B.aBA=s([B.aQm,B.aPP,B.aNU,B.aNp,B.aPp,B.aOy,B.aKb,B.aMy,B.aOO,B.aQd,B.aNn],t.E)
B.PH=new A.bk("chestpain","Cardiac Rehabilitation (Post-CABG)",B.aBA,!1)
B.aMh=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 4-year-old male presents with 'walking on his tiptoes'. He was born at 28 weeks gestation and spent 2 months in the NICU. His parents note his legs seem 'stiff' and 'scissor' when he runs.")
B.aQ4=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a child with toe walking and prematurity, suspect **Cerebral Palsy (CP)**.\n- **Birth History**: Gestational age? Intraventricular Hemorrhage (IVH)? Periventricular Leukomalacia (PVL)? (PVL affects leg fibers -> Diplegia).\n- **Development**: Age of walking? (Delayed >18 mos?).\n- **Function**: Can he climb stairs? Jump? (GMFCS Classification).\n- **Review of Systems**: Seizures? Vision (Strabismus)? Swallowing (Dysphagia)?")
B.aNZ=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Tone**: Increased tone (Spasticity) in bilateral Gastrocnemius and Hamstrings (Modified Ashworth Scale 2-3). Upper extremities are minimally involved.\n- **Strength**: Weakness in Anterior Tibialis and Gluteus Maximus.\n- **Gait**: Bilateral equinus (toe walking), scissoring (adductor spasticity), and increased lumbar lordosis.\n- **ROM**: Popliteal Angle -50 degrees (Hamstring tightness). Ankle dorsiflexion -20 degrees.")
B.aMD=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The history (Prematurity/PVL) and exam (Spastic Diplegia > UEs) confirm **Cerebral Palsy**.")
B.aLf=new A.k("+ Classify this patient's Motor Function using the GMFCS.","**GMFCS Level I or II**:\n- **Level I**: Walks without limitations (can run/jump).\n- **Level II**: Walks with limitations (trouble on uneven terrain/crowds), but no assistive device.\n- **Level III**: Walks with an assistive device (Walker/Crutches).\n*This classification predicts future motor potential and hip risk.*")
B.aNl=new A.k("+ Differentiate 'Jump Gait' vs. 'Crouch Gait' in CP.","- **Jump Gait**: Equinus (Toe down) + Knee Extension + Hip Flexion. (Looks like jumping into water).\n- **Crouch Gait**: Excessive Dorsiflexion + Knee Flexion + Hip Flexion. (often iatrogenic from over-lengthening the heel cord). \n*Management implies different muscle targets (Gastroc vs. Hamstrings/Psoas).*")
B.aKQ=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You are managing his spasticity to improve gait efficiency.")
B.aPE=new A.k("+ What are the options for focal vs. generalized spasticity?","1.  **Focal (Botulinum Toxin)**: Best for specific muscles (e.g., Gastrocnemius for toe walking). Goal: Delay surgery.\n2.  **Generalized (Oral Meds)**: Baclofen/Diazepam. (Side effect: Sedation).\n3.  **Surgical (SDR)**: Selective Dorsal Rhizotomy (Permanent reduction in tone). Best for GMFCS I-III with pure spasticity (no dystonia).\n4.  **Intrathecal Baclofen (Pump)**: Usually for severe spasticity (GMFCS IV-V).")
B.aPU=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You order a pelvic X-ray. The radiologist mentions the 'Migration Percentage'.")
B.aPe=new A.k("+ Explain the Hip Surveillance Guidelines for CP.","Children with CP are at high risk for **Hip Displacement** (Silent dislocation).\n- **Metric**: **Migration Percentage (MP)** = % of the femoral head uncovered by the acetabulum.\n- **Threshold**: **MP > 30%** is 'At Risk'. **MP > 40%** usually triggers orthopedic referral for soft tissue release or osteotomy.\n- **Protocol**: \n    - GMFCS I-II: X-ray at age 2 and 6.\n    - GMFCS III-V: X-ray **Every 6-12 months** until skeletal maturity.")
B.aPg=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: The mother asks: 'Is this my fault? Did I do something wrong during pregnancy?'")
B.aM0=new A.k("+ Manage this parental guilt.","1.  **Absolution**: 'This is absolutely **not your fault**. CP is caused by events often beyond anyone's control (like prematurity or vascular changes in the womb).'\n2.  **Focus on Potential**: 'What matters now is that his brain is amazing and plastic. Our therapies are designed to teach his brain new ways to move. You are doing everything right by bringing him here.'")
B.aDD=s([B.aMh,B.aQ4,B.aNZ,B.aMD,B.aLf,B.aNl,B.aKQ,B.aPE,B.aPU,B.aPe,B.aPg,B.aM0],t.E)
B.PX=new A.bk("toewalking","Cerebral Palsy (Spastic Diplegia)",B.aDD,!1)
B.aQc=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 68-year-old male presents with 'clumsy hands' and difficulty walking after a hyperextension injury (fall forward striking his chin). He has a history of Cervical Stenosis.")
B.aL0=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In an elderly patient with a fall, this is **Central Cord Syndrome (CCS)** until proven otherwise.\n- **Mechanism**: Hyperextension in a stenotic canal -> pinches the cord centrally.\n- **Symptoms**: **'Man in a Barrel'** presentation.\n    - **Upper Extremities**: Severe weakness (LMN signs at level, UMN below).\n    - **Lower Extremities**: Mild weakness or spasticity (often can walk).\n    - **Sensory**: Variable sensory loss below the level.\n    - **Bladder**: Urinary retention is common initially.")
B.aP4=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Motor**: \n    - Deltoids/Biceps: 4/5.\n    - **Hands (Intrinsics)**: 1/5 (Corticospinal tracts to hands are medial/central -> Most affected).\n    - Legs (Quads/PF): 4+/5 (Lumbar tracts are lateral -> Spared).\n- **Sensory**: Decreased pinprick in hands ('Cape-like' distribution if syrinx, but here it's edema).\n- **Reflexes**: Hyperreflexic in legs (Babinski +), Hyporeflexic in arms (at level).")
B.aQg=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture of **Hands > Legs weakness** following hyperextension confirms **Central Cord Syndrome**.")
B.aKP=new A.k("+ An MRI shows C3-C4 cord edema and stenosis but no fracture. Neurosurgery declines surgery initially. 24 hours later, his hand strength drops to 0/5. What is the management?","**Urgent Surgical Decompression**.\n- **Guideline**: While CCS was historically treated conservatively, modern evidence (STASCIS trial rationale) supports **Early Decompression (<24 hours)** for patients with *progressive* neurologic deficit or continuing compression.\n- **Goal**: Prevent secondary ischemia and permanent hand paralysis.")
B.aOo=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","He undergoes C3-C4 ACDF. He is now in rehab.")
B.aOP=new A.k("+ Describe the expected Pattern of Recovery for Central Cord Syndrome.","**'Legs -> Bladder -> Hands'**.\n1.  **Lower Extremities**: Recover first. Prognosis for ambulation is **Good** (>75% will walk).\n2.  **Bladder**: Control usually returns.\n3.  **Upper Extremities (Hands)**: Recover **LAST** and often **Incompletely**. Fine motor dexterity is the major long-term deficit.")
B.aQk=new A.k("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You are prescribing a power wheelchair. Medicare denies it because he can walk 10 feet with a walker.")
B.aNw=new A.k("+ How do you advocate for the chair based on 'MRADL' criteria?","Medicare covers mobility devices for **Mobility-Related Activities of Daily Living (MRADLs)** (Toileting, Feeding, Dressing) *in the home*.\n- **Argument**: While he can *physically* walk 10 feet (Ambulation), his **Central Cord hands** prevent him from safely using a walker (Grip strength 1/5) or performing MRADLs safely. The chair is for **Safety** and acts as a platform for his ADLs, not just transport. Walking 10 feet is not 'functional household ambulation'.")
B.aPH=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: He is frustrated. 'I can walk to the bathroom, but I can't unzip my pants or wipe myself. I feel useless. Why can't I just use my hands?'")
B.aPr=new A.k("+ Manage this frustration regarding the specific 'Hand' deficit.","1.  **Validate**: 'It is incredibly frustrating. In Central Cord injury, the hands are the last to wake up because those nerve fibers are right in the center of the swelling.'\n2.  **Adaptive Strategy**: 'While we wait for the nerves to heal, we have tools to give you dignity back today. I will have OT give you a **zipper pull**, **elastic waistbands**, and a **toilet aid**. We don't stop rehab; we adapt so you can be independent *now* while we work on the cure.'")
B.aD8=s([B.aQc,B.aL0,B.aP4,B.aQg,B.aKP,B.aOo,B.aOP,B.aQk,B.aNw,B.aPH,B.aPr],t.E)
B.PB=new A.bk("handclumsiness","Central Cord Syndrome (SCI)",B.aD8,!1)
B.aKE=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 45-year-old female presents with severe, burning pain in her right hand. Six weeks ago, she suffered a distal radius fracture (Colles Fracture) which was treated with casting. The cast was removed 1 week ago, but she reports the hand is now 'on fire', swollen, and turns red when hanging down.")
B.aMw=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","Suspect **CRPS Type 1** (Reflex Sympathetic Dystrophy) after trauma.\n- **Pain Quality**: Burning, throbbing, or shooting? (Neuropathic).\n- **Allodynia**: Does light touch (clothing/wind) cause pain?\n- **Autonomic**: Changes in color (red/blue), temperature (hot/cold), or sweating?\n- **Trophic**: Rapid nail growth? Hair changes?\n- **Red Flag**: Rule out **Acute Infection** (Cellulitis/Osteomyelitis) or **DVT**.")
B.aNP=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Inspection**: Right hand is edematous and erythematous compared to the left. nails are brittle.\n- **Palpation**: Hand is warm (+2 degrees vs left). Extreme allodynia to light touch.\n- **Motor**: Weakness due to pain. Tremor present.\n- **ROM**: Decreased active ROM in fingers due to edema.")
B.aL6=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The constellation of burning pain, autonomic dysfunction, and trophic changes post-fracture meets the **Budapest Criteria** for CRPS.")
B.aPf=new A.k("+ Define the 'Budapest Criteria' for clinical diagnosis.","Diagnosing CRPS requires **Continuing Pain disproportionate to the event** PLUS:\n1.  **Sensory**: Hyperesthesia/Allodynia.\n2.  **Vasomotor**: Temperature/Color asymmetry.\n3.  **Sudomotor/Edema**: Sweating asymmetry or Edema.\n4.  **Motor/Trophic**: Decreased ROM, weakness, tremor, or nail/hair changes.\n*Must have at least 1 symptom in 3/4 categories and 1 sign in 2/4 categories.*")
B.aOl=new A.k("+ You suspect CRPS. Is a Triple Phase Bone Scan useful?","**Yes**, but timing matters.\n- **Acute Phase (<6 months)**: Highly sensitive. Shows diffuse uptake in the perfusion, blood pool, and delayed phases (periarticular uptake).\n- **Chronic Phase**: May be normal or show decreased uptake ('Cold' CRPS).")
B.aLE=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You initiate management: 'Desensitization' physical therapy and Medications.")
B.aLa=new A.k("+ Describe 'Desensitization Protocol' and Medical options.","1.  **Desensitization**: Rubbing the affected limb with progressively coarser textures (silk -> wool -> towel) to retrain the central sensory cortex and reduce allodynia.\n2.  **Meds**: **Gabapentin/Pregabalin** (Neuropathic pain), **Nifedipine** (Vasodilator for cold/ischemic CRPS), **Bisphosphonates** (for bone pain/osteopenia), and **Vitamin C** (Prevention post-fracture).")
B.aMz=new A.k("+ The patient fails conservative therapy after 4 weeks. What is the next Interventional step for Upper Extremity CRPS?","**Stellate Ganglion Block (SGB)**.\n- **Target**: Sympathetic chain at C6/C7 (Chassaignac's tubercle).\n- **Sign of Success**: **Horner's Syndrome** (Ptosis, Miosis, Anhidrosis) indicates a successful sympathetic blockade.\n- **Goal**: Break the sympathetic feedback loop to allow PT participation.")
B.aPa=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: 'My surgeon thinks I'm crazy. He says the X-ray is fine and I'm just looking for drugs. Is this all in my head?'")
B.aKC=new A.k("+ Manage this medical gaslighting.","1.  **Validation**: 'This is absolutely **Real**. It is a biological malfunction of the nervous system, not a psychological problem. You are not crazy.'\n2.  **Education**: 'The nerves that control blood flow and sensation are stuck in an 'alarm' mode. We can see the swelling and temperature change\u2014that is objective proof. We will treat this like any other nerve injury.'")
B.aDI=s([B.aKE,B.aMw,B.aNP,B.aL6,B.aPf,B.aOl,B.aLE,B.aLa,B.aMz,B.aPa,B.aKC],t.E)
B.PD=new A.bk("burninghandpain","Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS Type 1)",B.aDI,!1)
B.aL9=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 52-year-old female presents with 'stiff hands' upon waking that last for 2 hours. She also reports vague neck pain and 'electric shocks' down her spine when she looks down (Lhermitte's sign). She has a history of seropositive RA.")
B.aQi=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In a patient with RA and neck symptoms, you must rule out **Cervical Myelopathy**.\n- **RA Specifics**: Duration of disease? Current meds? (DMARDs/Biologics).\n- **Morning Stiffness**: >1 hour is classic for inflammatory arthritis (vs. OA which is <30 mins).\n- **Neurologic**: Clumsy hands? Gait instability? Bowel/Bladder changes?\n- **Red Flags**: **Lhermitte's Sign** suggests cord compression.")
B.aQC=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Hands**: Ulnar deviation at MCPs. Swan-neck deformities. Synovitis (boggy swelling) in MCPs/PIPs.\n- **Neck**: Limited ROM in rotation.\n- **Neurologic**: Hyperreflexia in legs (Babinski +). Hoffman's sign positive bilaterally. (LMN signs at level, UMN below).\n- **Skin**: Rheumatoid nodules on elbows.")
B.aL_=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of RA, Lhermitte's sign, and UMN signs suggests **Atlanto-Axial Subluxation** with myelopathy.")
B.aKS=new A.k("+ Describe the ACR/EULAR 2010 Criteria for RA Diagnosis.","Diagnosis requires a score of **\u22656/10**:\n1.  **Joint Involvement**: More small joints = higher score (0-5).\n2.  **Serology**: RF or Anti-CCP (+ = 2, High + = 3).\n3.  **Acute Phase Reactants**: CRP or ESR (+ = 1).\n4.  **Duration**: \u22656 weeks (+ = 1).")
B.aQb=new A.k("+ What is the specific life-threatening cervical spine pathology in RA?","**Atlanto-Axial Instability (AAI)**.\n- **Pathology**: Erosion of the Transverse Ligament allows the Dens (C2) to compress the spinal cord against the C1 arch.\n- **Screening**: **Flexion/Extension X-rays** (Lateral view).\n- **Measurement**: **Anterior Atlanto-Dental Interval (AADI)**. >3.5mm suggests instability. >9-10mm or **Posterior (PADI)** <14mm usually requires surgery.")
B.aOL=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","X-rays confirm AADI of 8mm. MRI shows cord compression.")
B.aMf=new A.k("+ What is the management of RA Cervical Instability?","**Surgical Decompression and Fusion**.\n- **Procedure**: Occipito-Cervical Fusion or C1-C2 Fusion.\n- **Contraindication**: **Do NOT manipulate** the neck. Airway management for anesthesia must use fiberoptic intubation to avoid hyperextension.")
B.aP6=new A.k("+ How do you manage her medical treatment (DMARDs) perioperatively?","1.  **Methotrexate**: generally **continued** (Safe, reduced flare risk).\n2.  **Biologics (TNF-inhibitors)**: **Hold** for 1 dosing cycle prior to surgery (due to infection risk) and resume after wound healing (approx. 14 days).")
B.aPB=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: 'I just want a chiropractic adjustment for my neck pain. Why are you saying I need surgery?'")
B.aKJ=new A.k("+ Counsel this patient on the risk of high-velocity manipulation.","1.  **Direct Warning**: 'With your Rheumatoid Arthritis, the ligament holding the top of your spine is damaged. A chiropractic adjustment (high-velocity thrust) could snap that ligament and cause permanent paralysis or even death.'\n2.  **Alternative**: 'We can treat the muscle pain with gentle soft tissue work and heat, but we must protect the bones until the surgeon stabilizes them.'")
B.aC3=s([B.aL9,B.aQi,B.aQC,B.aL_,B.aKS,B.aQb,B.aOL,B.aMf,B.aP6,B.aPB,B.aKJ],t.E)
B.Pv=new A.bk("morningstiffness","Rheumatoid Arthritis (Cervical Instability)",B.aC3,!1)
B.aOE=new A.k("Initial Presentation","A 35-year-old construction worker presents to clinic. 4 weeks ago, he suffered a traumatic crushing injury to his right forearm, requiring a Transradial Amputation. He is right-hand dominant and desperate to get back to work.")
B.aON=new A.k("History & Systems Review:","In upper limb amputation, the **Vocational Goals** drive the prescription.\n- **Job Demands**: Heavy lifting? Dust/dirt exposure? Fine motor?\n- **Dominance**: Was it the dominant hand? (transfer of dominance takes time).\n- **ADLs**: Can he feed/dress himself with the left hand?\n- **Pain**: Screen for Phantom Limb Pain vs. Neuroma.\n- **Golden Window**: It has been 4 weeks. We must fit him **NOW**.")
B.aQ2=new A.k("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Residual Limb**: Well-healed, cylindrical shape. Length is mid-forearm (ideal). No bony prominence.\n- **ROM**: Full Elbow flexion/extension.\n- **Strength**: Good pronation/supination intact.\n- **Contralateral Limb**: Check for signs of overuse (Carpal tunnel, rotator cuff tenderness) as he is one-handed.")
B.aOR=new A.k("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","He requests a 'Robotic Hand' (Myoelectric) because it looks real. However, he works in a dusty environment lifting heavy beams.")
B.aPG=new A.k("+ Compare Body-Powered vs. Myoelectric for this specific patient.","For a **Heavy Duty Construction Worker**, a **Body-Powered** system is superior:\n1.  **Body-Powered (Cable)**: **Durable** (can take a beating), **Proprioception** (can feel tension on the cable), **Lighter**, and field-repairable.\n2.  **Myoelectric**: **Fragile** (dust/dirt can damage motors), **Heavy**, and requires charging. Battery usually lasts 1 day. Better for office work/cosmesis.")
B.aNt=new A.k("+ Prescribe the specific components.","1.  **Socket**: Supracondylar suspension (self-suspending).\n2.  **Control**: Figure-of-8 Harness (Cable control).\n3.  **Terminal Device**: **Voluntary Opening (VO) Split Hook**.\n    - *Why a Hook?* It allows fine precision (holding a nail) and visualizing the object, which a prosthetic hand blocks. It is also durable for tools.\n    - *Compromise*: Prescribe a **Myoelectric Hand** as a secondary device for social events (Church/Dinner).")
B.aOS=new A.k("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","He is fitted within 30 days ('Golden Window'). 3 months later, he complains of Left Shoulder pain (the uninjured side).")
B.aNr=new A.k("+ Diagnosis and Pathophysiology?","**Overuse Syndrome** of the Intact Limb.\n- **Mechanism**: The sound limb performs 100% of ADLs plus assists the prosthetic limb. \n- **Management**: This is arguably MORE important than the amputation rehab. If his 'good' arm fails, he is totally dependent.\n- **Rx**: OT for joint conservation, and ensure the prosthesis is actually functional (so he uses it).")
B.aMG=new A.k(u.j,"Role-Play: He looks at the Hook device and says: 'I look like Captain Hook. I can't wear this in public. People will stare.'")
B.aPD=new A.k("+ Address this Body Image conflict.","1.  **Acknowledge**: 'I hear you. The hook is amazing for work, but it stands out socially.'\n2.  **The 'Tool' Metaphor**: 'Think of the hook like a specialized tool, a wrench or a hammer. You use it to get the job done. But you don't wear your toolbelt to dinner.'\n3.  **Solution**: 'That is why we will try to get you a **Passive Cosmetic Hand** or a Myoelectric hand for social situations, so you feel confident in public, while staying safe at work.'")
B.aCd=s([B.aOE,B.aON,B.aQ2,B.aOR,B.aPG,B.aNt,B.aOS,B.aNr,B.aMG,B.aPD],t.E)
B.PC=new A.bk("workaccident","Prosthetics (Upper Limb Amputation)",B.aCd,!1)
